In Linux, the `div_u64()` function is typically implemented in the `include/linux/kernel.h` header file.

Here is an example implementation of `div_u64()` in C:

```c
#include <linux/kernel.h>

u64 div_u64(u64 dividend, u32 divisor)
{
    u64 quotient;

    asm("divq %%rcx;"
        : "=a" (quotient)
        : "a" ((u32)(dividend >> 32)), "d" ((u32)dividend), "c" (divisor)
        : "cc");

    return quotient;
}
```

In this implementation, the function takes a 64-bit dividend (`dividend`) and a 32-bit divisor (`divisor`) as input. The quotient is stored in the 64-bit variable `quotient`, which is then returned.

The inline assembly code is used to perform the actual division operation using the `divq` instruction. The high 32 bits of the dividend are stored in the `edx` register (`d`), the low 32 bits are stored in the `eax` register (`a`), and the divisor is stored in the `ecx` register (`c`). The resulting quotient is stored back into `eax` (`a`).

The `cc` code constraint tells the compiler that the condition flags (e.g., overflow flag) may have been modified by the division operation.

Note that the actual implementation may vary slightly depending on the specific version of the Linux kernel and the architecture it is running on.

