In Java, you can use the `StringEscapeUtils` class from the Apache Commons Text library to escape or unescape special characters in a string. Specifically, if you want to escape the special character "&" as "&amp;", you can use the `escapeXml11` method provided by `StringEscapeUtils`. Here's an example:

```java
import org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "This &amp; That";
        String escaped = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(input);
        System.out.println(escaped); // Output: This &amp;amp; That

        // Unescaping the escaped string
        String unescaped = StringEscapeUtils.unescapeXml(escaped);
        System.out.println(unescaped); // Output: This &amp; That
    }
}
```

In this example, we first use `escapeXml11` to escape the special character "&" in the input string. The resulting string is then printed as "This &amp;amp; That". To unescape the escaped string, we can use `unescapeXml` which converts "&amp;" back to "&".

Make sure to include the Apache Commons Text library in your project's dependencies to use `StringEscapeUtils`. You can add the following dependency in your Maven `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
    <artifactId>commons-text</artifactId>
    <version>1.9</version>
</dependency>
```

Or you can manually download the library from the Apache Commons Text website and add it to your project's classpath.

