To get the data in your React component using Axios, you can make an HTTP request in a lifecycle method like `componentDidMount` and update the component's state with the received data. Here's an example of how you can achieve that:

1. Install Axios in your React project by running the following command:
```bash
npm install axios
```

2. Import Axios in your React component file:
```javascript
import axios from 'axios';
```

3. Define your React component and add the `componentDidMount` method. Inside this method, make the HTTP request using Axios:
```javascript
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import axios from 'axios';

class YourComponent extends Component {
  state = {
    task: '',
    stattxt: ''
  };

  componentDidMount() {
    axios.get('/your-controller-route') // Replace 'your-controller-route' with the actual URL of your controller endpoint
      .then(response => {
        const data = response.data;
        this.setState({ task: data.task, stattxt: data.stattxt });
      })
      .catch(error => {
        console.log(error);
      });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <div>Task: {this.state.task}</div>
        <div>Status Text: {this.state.stattxt}</div>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default YourComponent;
```

Make sure to replace `'your-controller-route'` with the actual URL or route of your controller endpoint. Also, update the rendering logic inside the `render` method according to your needs.

This code will make an HTTP GET request to your controller's route, receive the data returned from the controller, and update the component's state with the received data. The updated state values will then be rendered in your component.

