To disable verification when using libcurl, you can use the CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER and CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST options with the curl_easy_setopt() function. Here's an example:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <curl/curl.h>

int main() {
    CURL* curl = curl_easy_init();
    if (curl) {
        // Disable peer verification
        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER, 0L);

        // Disable hostname verification
        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST, 0L);

        // Rest of the curl_easy_setopt() calls

        // Execute the request
        CURLcode res = curl_easy_perform(curl);

        // Check for errors
        if (res != CURLE_OK) {
            std::cerr << "Request failed: " << curl_easy_strerror(res) << std::endl;
        }

        // Cleanup
        curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, notice that CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER is set to 0 to disable peer verification, and CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST is set to 0 to disable hostname verification. However, keep in mind that disabling these verifications can expose you to potential security risks.

