What causes the following code to slow down very much after the server receives more than 5 messages in a short time:

func HandleConnectionRead(connection net.Conn, server *Server) {
	id := uuid.Must(uuid.NewRandom()).String()
	client, err := NewClient(connection, id)
	if err != nil {
		connection.Close()
		log.Error().Err(err).Msg("unable to create client")
		return
	}

	// add client to store
	server.clients.Add(client)

	// starts goroutines for procesing incoming and outgoing messages
	go HandleConnectionOut(client, server)
	go HandleConnectionIn(client, server)

	// read input from client
	reader := bufio.NewReader(client.connection)
	for {
		log.Debug().Msgf("Buffer size: %d", reader.Size())
		line, err := reader.ReadString('\n')
		now := time.Now()

		if err != nil {
			log.Error().Err(err).Msgf("unable to read from client (%s)", client.connection.RemoteAddr())
			break
		}

		line = strings.Trim(line, "\r\n")

		// PING
		// https://modern.ircdocs.horse/#ping-message
		// https://modern.ircdocs.horse/#pong-message
		if strings.HasPrefix(line, "PING") {
			client.send <- strings.Replace(line, "PING", "PONG", 1)
			continue
		}

		// send to receive channel
		client.recv <- line
		took := time.Since(now)
		log.Debug().Msgf("Took: %f s to read message", took.Seconds())
	}

	// cleanup
	err = client.Close()
	if err != nil {
		log.Error().Err(err).Msg("unable to close client on write handler")
	}
	err = server.RemoveClient(client)
	if err != nil {
		log.Error().Err(err).Msg("unable to remove client on write handler")
	}
}

If the `reader.ReadString('\n')` function is blocking and causing the slowdown, the most likely reason is that the server is receiving messages faster than it can process them. The blocking call to `reader.ReadString()` will wait until it receives a newline character or an error, and during this time, the server is unable to handle any other incoming messages.

One solution to overcome this issue is to introduce concurrency and parallelize the processing of incoming messages. One way to achieve this is by using goroutines to handle each message concurrently. For example, you can create a new goroutine for each message received and process it independently:

```go
line, err := reader.ReadString('\n')
if err != nil {
    // handle the error
}

line = strings.Trim(line, "\r\n")

go func() {
    // process the message in a separate goroutine
    // this can include parsing, validation, and any other necessary operations
    processMessage(line)

    // perform any necessary cleanup after processing the message
}()

```

By utilizing goroutines for message processing, the server can continue to handle incoming messages without getting blocked by the call to `reader.ReadString()`. This will allow the server to handle a larger volume of messages efficiently and reduce the overall slowdown.

