To calculate the number of ways processes can be scheduled, we can use dynamic programming.

First, we need to create a 2D array dp with n_intervals+1 rows and n_processes+1 columns. We initialize all elements of the array to 0.

The dp[i][j] represents the number of ways processes can be scheduled up to the i-th interval using the first j processes.

We can fill the dp array using the following rules:

1. dp[0][j] = 1 for all j from 0 to n_processes. This means there is only one way to schedule processes when there are no intervals.

2. dp[i][0] = 0 for all i from 1 to n_intervals. This means there are no processes to schedule.

3. For each dp[i][j], we have two options:
   a. We can skip processing the j-th process for the i-th interval. In this case, dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1].
   b. We can process the j-th process for the i-th interval. In this case, dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1].

Finally, the answer will be dp[n_intervals][n_processes] modulo 10^9 + 7.

Here is the implementation in Java:

```java
public static int processScheduling(int n_intervals, int n_processes) {
    int[][] dp = new int[n_intervals+1][n_processes+1];
    int mod = 1000000007;

    for (int i = 0; i <= n_intervals; i++) {
        dp[i][0] = 0;
    }

    for (int j = 0; j <= n_processes; j++) {
        dp[0][j] = 1;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= n_intervals; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n_processes; j++) {
            dp[i][j] = (dp[i][j-1] + dp[i-1][j-1]) % mod;
        }
    }

    return dp[n_intervals][n_processes];
}
```

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n_intervals * n_processes) and a space complexity of O(n_intervals * n_processes) since we are using a 2D array to store the intermediate results.

Apologies for the mistake. The current implementation doesn't handle the case correctly when n_intervals is less than or equal to n_processes.

To fix it, we need to handle this special case separately. When n_intervals is less than or equal to n_processes, the answer should be n_intervals!. In our case, we can calculate n_intervals! using a separate helper function that calculates the factorial of a number.

Here's the updated implementation:

```java
public static int processScheduling(int n_intervals, int n_processes) {
    if (n_intervals <= n_processes) {
        return factorial(n_intervals);
    }

    int[][] dp = new int[n_intervals+1][n_processes+1];
    int mod = 1000000007;

    for (int i = 0; i <= n_intervals; i++) {
        dp[i][0] = 0;
    }

    for (int j = 0; j <= n_processes; j++) {
        dp[0][j] = 1;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= n_intervals; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n_processes; j++) {
            dp[i][j] = (dp[i][j-1] + dp[i-1][j-1]) % mod;
        }
    }

    return dp[n_intervals][n_processes];
}

public static int factorial(int n) {
    int result = 1;
    int mod = 1000000007;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        result = (result * i) % mod;
    }

    return result;
}
```

