Here is an example of how to use Firebase Functions to dispatch tasks:

1. First, make sure you have the Firebase CLI installed and are logged in to your Firebase account.

2. Create a new Firebase project if you haven't already done so.

3. Open a terminal and navigate to the root directory of your project.

4. Run the following command to initialize Firebase Functions:

   ```
   firebase init functions
   ```

   This command will set up a new "functions" directory in your project, which will contain all your function files.

5. Change your working directory to the newly created "functions" directory:

   ```
   cd functions
   ```

6. Install the necessary dependencies for Firebase Functions:

   ```
   npm install firebase-functions
   ```

7. Open the "index.js" file in the "functions" directory and add the following code to define your Firebase Function:

   ```javascript
   const functions = require('firebase-functions');

   exports.dispatchTask = functions.https.onRequest((req, res) => {
     // Your task dispatching code here

     // For example, you can send a task to a worker by making an HTTP request
     // to another API endpoint:
     fetch('https://example.com/dispatch', {
       method: 'POST',
       body: JSON.stringify({ task: 'exampleTask' }),
       headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
     })
       .then(response => response.json())
       .then(data => {
         // Process the response from the worker
         console.log(data);
         res.status(200).send('Task dispatched successfully');
       })
       .catch(error => {
         console.error('Error dispatching task:', error);
         res.status(500).send('Error dispatching task');
       });
   });
   ```

   This example shows how to send a task to a worker by making a POST request to an API endpoint. You can modify this code to suit your specific requirements.

8. Deploy the Firebase Function to your Firebase project by running the following command:

   ```
   firebase deploy --only functions
   ```

   This command will upload your function code to Firebase and make it accessible via an HTTPS endpoint.

9. After the deployment is successful, you can access your Firebase Function by using the provided HTTPS endpoint. For example:

   ```
   https://<your-project-id>.cloudfunctions.net/dispatchTask
   ```

   Whenever a request is made to this endpoint, the task dispatching code will be executed, sending a task to the worker.

That's it! You have successfully set up and deployed a Firebase Function to dispatch tasks.

