To convert between host endian and little endian in C language, you can use the following code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Convert host endian to little endian
unsigned int hostToLittleEndian(unsigned int num) {
    unsigned char *p = (unsigned char *)&num;
    unsigned int result = ((unsigned int)p[3] << 24) | ((unsigned int)p[2] << 16) |
                          ((unsigned int)p[1] << 8) | p[0];
    return result;
}

// Convert little endian to host endian
unsigned int littleEndianToHost(unsigned int num) {
    unsigned char *p = (unsigned char *)&num;
    unsigned int result = ((unsigned int)p[0] << 24) | ((unsigned int)p[1] << 16) |
                          ((unsigned int)p[2] << 8) | p[3];
    return result;
}

int main() {
    unsigned int num = 0x12345678;  // Example value

    printf("Original number: 0x%08x\n", num);

    unsigned int littleEndianNum = hostToLittleEndian(num);
    printf("Little endian number: 0x%08x\n", littleEndianNum);

    unsigned int hostEndianNum = littleEndianToHost(littleEndianNum);
    printf("Host endian number: 0x%08lx\n", hostEndianNum);

    return 0;
}
```

This code includes two functions: `hostToLittleEndian` and `littleEndianToHost`.

The `hostToLittleEndian` function takes a number in host endian and converts it to little endian by manipulating the individual bytes.

The `littleEndianToHost` function does the opposite, taking a number in little endian and converting it to host endian.

In the `main` function, an example value is used to demonstrate the conversion. The original number is printed, then `hostToLittleEndian` function is called to convert it to little endian, and the resulting number is printed. Then, `littleEndianToHost` is used to convert the number back to host endian, and the result is printed.

