  {
      return $this->username;
  }
  ```

By following the steps above, you will be able to authenticate users using their username instead of the default email-based authentication in your Laravel API using Passport.

If you want to supply a string to clap with a default value, you can use the `.default_value()` method provided by the clap library. Here's an example of how you can do that:

```rust
use clap::{App, Arg};

fn main() {
    let matches = App::new("MyApp")
        .arg(
            Arg::new("input")
                .about("input string")
                .required(false)
                .default_value("default value")
                .takes_value(true),
        )
        .get_matches();

    let input = matches.value_of("input").unwrap();
    println!("Input: {}", input);
}
```

In this example, the argument `input` is defined with `.default_value("default value")`. If no value is provided for `input` when running the program, it will default to `"default value"`.

To supply a default value of type `String` to `clap`, you can convert the default string value to a `String`. Here's an updated example:

```rust
use clap::{App, Arg};

fn main() {
    let default_value = "default value";
    let default_value_string = String::from(default_value);

    let matches = App::new("MyApp")
        .arg(
            Arg::new("input")
                .about("input string")
                .required(false)
                .default_value(default_value_string.as_str())
                .takes_value(true),
        )
        .get_matches();

    let input = matches.value_of("input").unwrap();
    println!("Input: {}", input);
}
```

In this example, the `default_value` variable is declared as a string literal. It is then converted to a `String` using `String::from()`. Then, when setting the default value for the `input` argument, you can use `default_value_string.as_str()` to pass the `&str` reference of the `String` value.

