```java
import java.lang.annotation.Annotation;

public boolean doesAnnotationExist(Class<?> clazz, Class<? extends Annotation> annotationClass) {
    // Check if the annotation is present in the given class
    if (clazz.isAnnotationPresent(annotationClass)) {
        return true;
    }

    // Check the annotation in the parent class (if exists)
    Class<?> superClass = clazz.getSuperclass();
    if (superClass != null) {
        return doesAnnotationExist(superClass, annotationClass);
    }

    return false;
}
```

In the above method, the `doesAnnotationExist` method takes two parameters - the `Class` object of the class to be checked and the `Class` object of the annotation. It first checks if the annotation is present in the given class using the `isAnnotationPresent` method. If the annotation is found, it returns `true`.

If the annotation is not found in the given class, it checks if the class has a parent class. If a parent class exists, it makes a recursive call to `doesAnnotationExist` with the parent class and the same annotation class. This recursive call continues until either the annotation is found in any of the ancestor classes or all the ancestor classes are checked.

If the annotation is not found in the class or any of its ancestor classes, the method returns `false`.

Note: This method uses Java reflection to perform the annotation check, so the class and the annotation class should be available during runtime.

