If both the start date and end date are the same in JavaScript, the date range will not work as expected. This is because the date range requires a minimum of two different dates to form a range.

Here is an example of how the date range will not work if both dates are the same:

```javascript
const startDate = new Date('2021-10-10');
const endDate = new Date('2021-10-10');

console.log(startDate); // Output: Sun Oct 10 2021 00:00:00 GMT+0530 (India Standard Time)
console.log(endDate); // Output: Sun Oct 10 2021 00:00:00 GMT+0530 (India Standard Time)

// Date range
const dateRange = endDate - startDate;
console.log(dateRange); // Output: 0
```

In the above example, both `startDate` and `endDate` are the same date '2021-10-10'. When calculating the date range by subtracting the start date from the end date, the result is zero. This happens because the range between two same dates is zero.

To make the date range work, you need to have at least two different dates. For example:

```javascript
const startDate = new Date('2021-10-10');
const endDate = new Date('2021-10-12');

console.log(startDate); // Output: Sun Oct 10 2021 00:00:00 GMT+0530 (India Standard Time)
console.log(endDate); // Output: Tue Oct 12 2021 00:00:00 GMT+0530 (India Standard Time)

// Date range
