This exception typically occurs when trying to start a new FFmpeg process when one is already running. 

To fix this issue, you need to ensure that the previous FFmpeg process has completed before starting a new one. You can achieve this by waiting for the previous process to exit before starting a new one.

Here is an example of how you can handle this situation in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Process ffmpegProcess = new Process();
        ffmpegProcess.StartInfo.FileName = "ffmpeg.exe";
        // Set other required options for FFmpeg process

        if (!ffmpegProcess.HasExited)
        {
            // Previous FFmpeg process is still running, wait for it to exit
            ffmpegProcess.WaitForExit();
        }

        // Start new FFmpeg process
        ffmpegProcess.Start();

        // Continue with rest of your code
    }
}
```

In this example, we check if the previous FFmpeg process is still running using the `HasExited` property. If it is still running, we wait for it to exit using the `WaitForExit` method. Once it has exited, we can start a new FFmpeg process.

By implementing this approach, you can avoid getting the "FFMpeg process is already started" exception.

