To display a string as "Input: " and position the cursor immediately after it without starting a new line, you can use the `print` method instead of the `println` method.

Here's an example of how you can achieve this in Java:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Input: "); // Use print instead of println

        // Read the user input
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        System.out.println("You entered: " + input);
    }
}
```

In this example, `System.out.print` is used to display the "Input: " string without starting a new line. After the user enters their input, the `nextLine` method of the `Scanner` class is used to read the input.

The result will be a prompt to the user saying "Input: " with the cursor positioned immediately after it, and the user's input will be displayed on the next line.

Apologies for the confusion. If you want to display the "Input: " string and have the cursor positioned immediately after it on the same line without a newline, you can use the `System.out.print` method to print the string followed by a `System.out.flush()` call to ensure it is immediately displayed:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Input: ");
        System.out.flush(); // Flush the output to immediately display

        String input = scanner.nextLine();
        System.out.println("You entered: " + input);
    }
}
```

In this updated example, `System.out.flush()` is called after `System.out.print` to ensure that the text is immediately displayed on the console.

Now, when you run the program, you will see the prompt "Input: " on the same line as the cursor.

