Here is a Java code example that generates a MIDI file with a drum pattern:

```java
import java.io.File;
import javax.sound.midi.*;

public class DrumPatternGenerator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Sequence sequence = new Sequence(Sequence.PPQ, 4);

            Track track = sequence.createTrack();

            // Add base pattern (first 16 steps)
            addDrumEvent(track, ShortMessage.NOTE_ON, 0, 42, 100, 0);
            for (int i = 1; i < 16; i++) {
                addDrumEvent(track, ShortMessage.NOTE_ON, 0, 42, 100, i);
            }

            // Randomly alter base pattern until 64 steps
            for (int i = 16; i < 64; i++) {
                if (Math.random() <= 0.1) {
                    int previousStep = (int) (Math.random() * i);
                    addDrumEvent(track, ShortMessage.NOTE_ON, 0, 42, 100, i);
                    addDrumEvent(track, ShortMessage.NOTE_OFF, 0, 42, 100, previousStep);
                } else {
                    addDrumEvent(track, ShortMessage.NOTE_ON, 0, 42, 100, i);
                }
            }

            // Set end of track
            track.add(new MetaMessage(47, new byte[] { 0 }, 1), sequence.getTickLength());

            // Save the sequence into a MIDI file
            File file = new File("drum_pattern.midi");
            MidiSystem.write(sequence, 1, file);

            System.out.println("MIDI file generated successfully.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private static void addDrumEvent(Track track, int messageType, int channel, int note, int velocity, int tick) throws InvalidMidiDataException {
        ShortMessage message = new ShortMessage();
        message.setMessage(messageType, channel, note, velocity);
        track.add(new MidiEvent(message, tick));
    }
}
```

This code uses the Java Sound API to create a `Sequence` object, adds a `Track` to it, and then adds drum events (MIDI messages) to the track.

The drum pattern starts with a base pattern of 16 steps (each step plays the drum sound represented by MIDI note number 42 with a velocity of 100). Then, starting from the 17th step, each step has a 10% chance of randomly altering the previous step. The altered step will have a NOTE_ON event while the previous step will have a NOTE_OFF event to stop its sound.

Make sure you have the Java Sound API (javax.sound.midi) available in your project, and handle any exceptions that may occur during MIDI file generation.

Please note that the code snippet provided may have more or less than 64 steps depending on the number of iterations. You can change the loop condition `i < 64` to adjust the total number of steps generated.

