The request_threaded_irq function is used to request an interrupt line (IRQ) and associate it with a threaded handler.

Here is an example of how to use the request_threaded_irq function in the Linux kernel:

```c
irqreturn_t irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id)
{
    // Interrupt handling logic
    // ...

    return IRQ_HANDLED;
}

int init_module(void)
{
    int irq = 42;  // IRQ number
    int irq_flags = IRQF_SHARED;  // Flags for interrupt request

    // Request IRQ and associate it with the irq_handler
    int ret = request_threaded_irq(irq, irq_handler, NULL, irq_flags, "my_interrupt", NULL);
    if (ret != 0) {
        printk(KERN_INFO "Failed to request IRQ: %d\n", ret);
        return ret;
    }

    // Module initialization logic
    // ...

    return 0;
}

void cleanup_module(void)
{
    int irq = 42;  // IRQ number

    // Release the requested IRQ
    free_irq(irq, NULL);

    // Module cleanup logic
    // ...
}
```

In this example, the irq_handler function is the threaded interrupt handler that will be called when the associated IRQ is triggered. The init_module function requests the IRQ using the request_threaded_irq function and associates it with the irq_handler. The cleanup_module function releases the requested IRQ using the free_irq function.

The irq argument in the request_threaded_irq function is the IRQ number that you want to request. The irq_handler argument is the pointer to the interrupt handler function. The dev_id argument is a pointer to the device-specific data associated with the interrupt handler (can be NULL). The irq_flags argument is used to specify additional flags for the interrupt request (e.g., IRQF_SHARED for sharing the IRQ line). The last two arguments are used for thread handling, specifying the name of the thread and an optional private data pointer.

You should ensure that the interrupt handler executes quickly to avoid blocking the system. The return value of IRQ_HANDLED indicates that the interrupt was handled successfully.

