public List<Module> getModules(UUID referenceId, Module.Area area) {
    Map<String, Module> moduleMap = moduleRepository
        .findByReferenceIdAndArea(area.toString(), referenceId)
        .stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(
            m -> m.getId().toString(),
            Function.identity(),
            (existing, replacement) -> existing // Resolve potential conflicts by keeping the existing module
        ));

    Set<History> comments = historyRepository
        .findByModuleIdAndMedicalRecordUuidAndRootCommentIsTrue(moduleMap.keySet(), referenceId);

    comments.forEach(comment -> {
        String moduleId = comment.getModuleId().toString();
        Module module = moduleMap.get(moduleId);
        if (module != null) {
            module.getComments().add(comment);
        }
    });

    List<Module> modules = new ArrayList<>(moduleMap.values());
    applyLogicalRemovalOfModules(modules, area, referenceId);
    return modules;
}
```

In this optimized refactor, we make the following changes:

1. Instead of using `computeIfPresent` to update the module map, we iterate over the set of comments and directly add the comment to the module in the map using `module.getComments().add(comment)`. This eliminates the need for a lambda function in `computeIfPresent` and avoids unnecessary overhead.

2. We use a more efficient approach for creating the final list of modules. Instead of converting the values of the module map to a stream and then collecting them into a list, we use an `ArrayList` constructor with the collection of values as an argument. This avoids unnecessary stream operations and potentially improves performance and memory consumption.

3. We resolve potential conflicts in the `toMap` operation by keeping the existing module when encountering duplicate keys. This is achieved by passing a lambda function `(existing, replacement) -> existing` as the third argument to the `toMap` method.

These optimizations aim to improve the overall performance and memory consumption of the code while maintaining the same logic and functionality as the original implementation.

