To integrate Dagger2 into a Spring Boot application, you need to follow these steps:

1. Add the Dagger2 dependencies to your Gradle or Maven project:

For Gradle:
```
implementation 'com.google.dagger:dagger:2.x'
annotationProcessor 'com.google.dagger:dagger-compiler:2.x'
```

For Maven:
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.google.dagger</groupId>
    <artifactId>dagger</artifactId>
    <version>2.x</version>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.google.dagger</groupId>
    <artifactId>dagger-compiler</artifactId>
    <version>2.x</version>
    <optional>true</optional>
</dependency>
```

2. Create a `@Module` class to define your Dagger2 modules. In this example, let's create a module called `ApplicationModule`:

```java
@Module
public class ApplicationModule {
    @Provides
    public SomeDependency provideSomeDependency() {
        return new SomeDependency();
    }
}   
```

3. Create a `@Component` interface to define your Dagger2 component. In this example, let's create an interface called `ApplicationComponent`:

```java
@Component(modules = {ApplicationModule.class})
public interface ApplicationComponent {
    void inject(SomeClass someClass);
}
```

4. In your Spring Boot application class, create an instance of the `ApplicationComponent` and use it to inject the dependencies:

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class MySpringBootApplication implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Inject
    private SomeClass someClass;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MySpringBootApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) throws Exception {
        // Use the injected dependencies
        someClass.doSomething();
    }

    @Bean
    public ApplicationComponent applicationComponent() {
        return DaggerApplicationComponent.builder()
                .applicationModule(new ApplicationModule())
                .build();
    }
}
```

5. Annotate the classes that need dependency injection with `@Inject`, and ensure that they are included in the component's `inject` method:

```java
@Component(modules = {ApplicationModule.class})
public interface ApplicationComponent {
    void inject(SomeClass someClass);
}
```

With these steps, your Dagger2 dependencies should be integrated into your Spring Boot application.

To integrate Dagger2 into a Spring Boot controller, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a Dagger2 module for your controller dependencies. In this example, let's create a module called `ControllerModule`:

```java
@Module
public class ControllerModule {
    @Provides
    public SomeDependency provideSomeDependency() {
        return new SomeDependency();
    }
}
```

2. Add the `@Component` annotation to the Dagger2 component interface and specify the modules that will provide the dependencies for the controller:

```java
@Component(modules = {ControllerModule.class})
public interface ControllerComponent {
    void inject(YourController yourController);
}
```

3. In your controller class, annotate the fields that require injection with `@Inject`:

```java
@RestController
public class YourController {
    @Inject
    private SomeDependency someDependency;

    // Rest of the controller methods
}
```

4. Create a method in your Spring Boot application class to instantiate the Dagger2 component and invoke the injection:

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class MySpringBootApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MySpringBootApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public ControllerComponent controllerComponent() {
        return DaggerControllerComponent.builder()
                .controllerModule(new ControllerModule())
                .build();
    }

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // Inject dependencies into controller
        controllerComponent().inject(new YourController());
    }
}
```

With these steps, your Dagger2 dependencies will be integrated into your Spring Boot controller. The specified dependencies in the module will be provided and injected into the corresponding fields in the controller.

