Here's an example of a JavaScript function that takes a string, searches for time in AM/PM format, and replaces it with the converted time using the function "convertTime12to24":

```javascript
function convertTime12to24(time) {
  var hours = parseInt(time.substr(0, 2));
  var minutes = parseInt(time.substr(3, 2));
  var meridian = time.substr(6, 2).toUpperCase();

  if (meridian === 'PM' && hours !== 12) {
    hours += 12;
  } else if (meridian === 'AM' && hours === 12) {
    hours = 0;
  }

  return hours.toString().padStart(2, '0') + ':' + minutes.toString().padStart(2, '0');
}

function replaceTimeWithConverted(str) {
  var regex = /\b\d{1,2}:\d{2}\s?(AM|PM)\b/g;

  return str.replace(regex, function (match) {
    return convertTime12to24(match);
  });
}
```

To use this function, pass the string as an argument to `replaceTimeWithConverted()`, and it will return the string with the converted times replaced:

```javascript
var text = "I have a meeting at 10:30 AM and another one at 02:45 PM.";

var convertedText = replaceTimeWithConverted(text);
console.log(convertedText);
// Output: "I have a meeting at 10:30 and another one at 14:45."
```

Note that this implementation assumes the input string contains time in the format of "HH:MM AM/PM" or "H:MM AM/PM", and the function handles conversion to the 24-hour format.

