To create a custom circle cursor in CSS that lowers the saturation of elements inside its area, you can utilize CSS variables, JavaScript, and event listeners to track the cursor position.

Here's an example:

HTML:
```html
<div class="circle-cursor"></div>
<!-- Your other elements -->
<div class="element1">Element 1</div>
<div class="element2">Element 2</div>
<!-- ... -->
```

CSS:
```css
:root {
  --cursor-size: 100px; /* Adjust the size of the cursor */
}

.circle-cursor {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  pointer-events: none; /* Allow elements underneath to be clickable */
  z-index: 9999;
  width: var(--cursor-size);
  height: var(--cursor-size);
  border-radius: 50%;
  border: 2px solid #000;
  mix-blend-mode: multiply;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}

/* Additional styling for demonstration purposes */
.element1 {
  background-color: red;
  color: white;
}

.element2 {
  background-color: blue;
  color: white;
}
```

JavaScript:
```js
const cursor = document.querySelector('.circle-cursor');

document.addEventListener('mousemove', (e) => {
  const { clientX, clientY } = e;
  const mouseX = clientX - window.innerWidth / 2;
  const mouseY = clientY - window.innerHeight / 2;
  cursor.style.transform = `translate(${mouseX}px, ${mouseY}px)`;
});
```

In this code, the `.circle-cursor` acts as the custom circle cursor. Adjust the `--cursor-size` variable to change the size of the cursor. The `position: fixed` ensures that the cursor stays in place even when scrolling.

The `mix-blend-mode` property allows us to manipulate the elements underneath. In this example, `multiply` is used to lower the saturation.

The JavaScript code tracks the mouse movement and updates the position of the `.circle-cursor` by modifying its `transform` property based on the mouse coordinates. It uses `event.clientX` and `event.clientY` to get the cursor's position.

Remember to adjust the HTML structure and CSS classes as per your specific needs. Feel free to modify the styles and behavior of the cursor to match your design aesthetic.

