import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.jdbc.datasource.DataSourceTransactionManager;

public class DataSourceTransactionManagerMockTest {

    @Test
    public void testCommitTransaction() {
        // Create a mock instance of DataSourceTransactionManager
        DataSourceTransactionManager dataSourceTransactionManagerMock = mock(DataSourceTransactionManager.class);

        // Mock the behavior of the commit method
        doNothing().when(dataSourceTransactionManagerMock).commit(any());

        // Call the method that uses DataSourceTransactionManager
        MyClass myClass = new MyClass(dataSourceTransactionManagerMock);
        myClass.doSomething();

        // Verify that the commit method was called once
        verify(dataSourceTransactionManagerMock, times(1)).commit(any());
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a mock instance of the DataSourceTransactionManager class using the `mock()` method from the Mockito library. We then mock the behavior of the `commit()` method using the `doNothing().when()` syntax, which means that the method will do nothing when called.

Next, we create an instance of `MyClass` and pass the mock `DataSourceTransactionManager` to its constructor. In `MyClass`, we call a method that uses the `DataSourceTransactionManager`, and finally, we verify that the `commit()` method was called once using the `verify()` method.

By mocking the behavior of the `DataSourceTransactionManager`, we can control its behavior in our unit tests and isolate the code under test from external dependencies.

To mock and test the transaction management with DataSourceTransactionManager in a Spring Boot application, you can use the Mockito and SpringBootTest framework. Here's an example:

1. Create a test class with the `@RunWith(SpringRunner.class)` and `@SpringBootTest` annotations to enable the Spring Boot test environment.

2. Use the `@MockBean` annotation to mock the DataSourceTransactionManager class.

3. In the test method, mock the behavior of the DataSourceTransactionManager class, perform the required transaction-related operations, and verify the expected behavior.

