No, you do not have to import all the modules into the entry point `index.js` file. It depends on your application's structure and requirements. 

If you have created separate modules for routes, controllers, and middleware, you can import and use them where they are needed. For example, if you have a `routes.js` file that exports a router middleware, you can import and use it in `index.js` like this:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const routes = require('./routes');

const app = express();
app.use(routes);

// ... other configuration and server setup
```

In this example, you are importing and using the `routes` module in the `index.js` file.

Similarly, you can import and use other modules where they are needed. By modularizing your app, you can selectively import and use the necessary components in different parts of your codebase rather than importing everything into the entry point file. This helps keep the codebase organized and improves maintainability.

Certainly! Here's a code example of a hypothetical `routes.js` module:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();

// Define your routes
router.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello, World!');
});

router.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  // logic to fetch and send users from database
});

router.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  // logic to create a new user in the database
});

// Export the router middleware
module.exports = router;
```

In this example, the `routes.js` module exports a router middleware using `module.exports = router;`. The module sets up various routes using the `router` object provided by Express. You can define your routes by calling methods like `get()`, `post()`, etc., on the `router` object.

Once you import this module into your `index.js` file as shown in the previous example, the routes defined in `routes.js` will be applied to your Express application using `app.use(routes)`. This way, you can keep your route handling logic separated into different modules, making it more modular and organized.

