The exception `javax.naming.NoInitialContextException` with the message `Need to specify class name in environment or system property, or in an application resource file: java.naming.factory.initial` typically occurs when the initial context factory class is not specified in the JNDI environment.

To fix this issue, you need to provide the initial context factory class name in your JNDI properties or environment. Here's an example of how you can set the `java.naming.factory.initial` property in your code:

```java
import java.util.Properties;
import javax.jms.ConnectionFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.jndi.JndiObjectFactoryBean;

@Configuration
public class JmsConfig {

    @Value("${connectionFactoryNames}")
    private String connectionFactoryNames;

    // ...

    @Bean(name = "tibJmsConnectionFactory")
    public ConnectionFactory tibJmsConnectionFactory() throws Exception {
        JndiObjectFactoryBean factoryBean = new JndiObjectFactoryBean();
        factoryBean.setJndiName(connectionFactoryNames);

        // Set the initial context factory class name
        Properties jndiProps = new Properties();
        jndiProps.put(Context.INITIAL_CONTEXT_FACTORY, "com.tibco.tibjms.naming.TibjmsInitialContextFactory");

        factoryBean.setJndiEnvironment(jndiProps);

        factoryBean.afterPropertiesSet();
        return (ConnectionFactory) factoryBean.getObject();
    }

    // ...
}
```

In the above code, the `java.naming.factory.initial` property is set to `"com.tibco.tibjms.naming.TibjmsInitialContextFactory"`. Adjust the class name according to the appropriate Tibco EMS configuration.

Make sure to include the necessary Tibco EMS library in your project's classpath for the initial context factory to be recognized.

