A promise-returning function is a function that returns a Promise object. Promises in JavaScript are used for handling asynchronous operations like HTTP requests, file I/O, etc.

On the other hand, a property that expects a void return means that it does not expect any return value. In JavaScript, the void keyword is used to indicate that a function does not return a value.

When a promise-returning function is provided to a property that expects a void return, it means that the property is expecting a synchronous function that does not return any value, but instead, the promise function returns a Promise object that resolves asynchronously.

For example, consider the following code snippet:

```javascript
function printMessage(message) {
  console.log(message);
}

function asyncPrintMessage(message) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      resolve(message);
    }, 1000);
  });
}

printMessage(asyncPrintMessage("Hello, world!"));
```

In the above code, the `printMessage` function expects a void return, but `asyncPrintMessage` returns a Promise object instead. When `asyncPrintMessage` is provided as an argument to `printMessage`, it does not resolve immediately, but returns a Promise object, which is not the expected behavior for `printMessage`.

To fix this issue, you can use the `then` method of the Promise object to handle the resolved value:

```javascript
asyncPrintMessage("Hello, world!").then(printMessage);
```

In this updated code, the `then` method is used to specify a callback function (`printMessage`) that will be executed when the Promise object resolves. This ensures that `printMessage` is only called with the resolved value, conforming to the expected behavior of the property.

To implement a Single Sign-On (SSO) WebAPI in C#, you can follow these steps:

1. Set up an authentication authority: You need to have an authentication authority in place, such as IdentityServer4 or Azure AD, to handle the authentication process. This authority will issue access tokens to your WebAPI.

2. Create a WebAPI project: Create a new C# WebAPI project using your preferred framework (e.g., ASP.NET Core, ASP.NET MVC). This project will contain the endpoints that need authentication for access.

3. Configure authentication middleware: Configure the authentication middleware in your WebAPI project's startup file. This middleware will validate the access tokens and authenticate the requests. Below is an example of configuring JWT bearer authentication in ASP.NET Core:

```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
        .AddJwtBearer(options =>
        {
            options.Authority = "https://your-authentication-authority.com";
            options.Audience = "your-api-audience";
        });

    services.AddControllers();
}

public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    app.UseAuthentication();
    app.UseAuthorization();

    app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllers();
    });
}
```

4. Protect the endpoints: Add the `[Authorize]` attribute to the controllers or actions that require authentication. This attribute will ensure that only authenticated requests can access those endpoints.

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class MyController : ControllerBase
{
    [Authorize]
    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult Get()
    {
        // Your code here
    }

    // Other actions
}
```

5. Test the SSO WebAPI: You can now test the SSO WebAPI by making requests to the protected endpoints. Ensure that you include the access token obtained from the authentication authority in the request headers.

These are the basic steps to implement a Single Sign-On WebAPI in C#. The specific implementation details may vary depending on the chosen authentication authority and framework.

