In ASP.NET applications, referer-based CSRF defenses can be implemented using web.config file settings. Here's an example of how to configure referer-based CSRF defense in web.config:

```xml
<system.web>
  <httpRuntime targetFramework="your-target-framework-version" requestValidationMode="your-request-validation-mode" />
  <pages validateRequest="true" />
  <httpModules>
    <add name="RefererModule" type="YourNamespace.RefererModule" />
  </httpModules>
  ...
</system.web>
```

In the above configuration, the `<httpRuntime>` and `<pages>` elements are used to enable request validation, which helps protect against various types of attacks, including CSRF. The `validateRequest` attribute is set to "true" to enable request validation.

The `<httpModules>` element is used to register a custom HTTP module, `RefererModule`, which can be created to implement referer-based CSRF defenses. The `type` attribute should be set to the fully qualified type name of your custom HTTP module.

Here's an example of how the custom `RefererModule` class can be implemented:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Web;

namespace YourNamespace
{
    public class RefererModule : IHttpModule
    {
        public void Init(HttpApplication context)
        {
            context.PreSendRequestHeaders += OnPreSendRequestHeaders;
        }

        public void Dispose()
        {
            // Clean up resources, if needed
        }

        private void OnPreSendRequestHeaders(object sender, EventArgs e)
        {
            if (HttpContext.Current.Request.UrlReferrer == null ||
                !HttpContext.Current.Request.UrlReferrer.Host.Equals(HttpContext.Current.Request.Url.Host, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            {
                // Reject the request or take appropriate action
                // For example, you can throw an exception, redirect to an error page, or simply return an error response.
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the example above, the `RefererModule` class implements the `IHttpModule` interface. In the `Init` method, the `PreSendRequestHeaders` event handler is registered to validate the referer header before sending the response.

The `OnPreSendRequestHeaders` method checks if the `UrlReferrer` property of the current request is null or if the host of the referrer does not match the host of the current request. If the checks fail, you can take appropriate action to reject or handle the request.

Once the custom HTTP module is defined, it can be registered in the web.config file as shown earlier.

It's worth noting that while referer-based CSRF defenses can provide an additional layer of protection, they should not be solely relied upon. Implementing other security measures like token-based CSRF prevention and secure coding practices is crucial to ensure the overall security of your web application.

