In JavaScript, static attributes are not inherited from the parent class to the child class. Static attributes are properties or variables defined directly on the class itself, rather than on instances of the class.

Here's an example to illustrate this:

```javascript
class Parent {
  static staticAttribute = "Parent static attribute";
}

class Child extends Parent { }

console.log(Parent.staticAttribute); // Output: "Parent static attribute"
console.log(Child.staticAttribute); // Output: undefined
```

In the example above, the `Parent` class has a static attribute `staticAttribute`. However, when we access `staticAttribute` on the `Child` class, it will return `undefined`, because static attributes are not inherited by the child class in JavaScript.

If you want the child class to inherit the static attributes from the parent class, you can manually set up the inheritance by copying the static attributes from the parent class to the child class. 

Here's an example of how you can achieve this using class inheritance and Object.assign():

```javascript
class Parent {
  static staticAttribute = "Parent static attribute";
