In Express, you can use the `res.redirect()` method to redirect to a 404 page. Here's an example:

```javascript
app.get('/invalid-url', (req, res) => {
  res.redirect('/404');
});

app.get('/404', (req, res) => {
  res.status(404).send('404 Not Found');
});
```

In this example, when accessing the `/invalid-url` route, it will be redirected to the `/404` route, which will respond with a 404 status code and the message "404 Not Found".

Alternatively, if you want to redirect all non-existent routes to a 404 page, you can use a wildcard router and define it as the last route:

```javascript
app.get('*', (req, res) => {
  res.redirect('/404');
});

// Define the 404 route
app.get('/404', (req, res) => {
  res.status(404).send('404 Not Found');
});
```

In this case, any route that doesn't match the defined routes will be redirected to the `/404` route, which will respond with a 404 status code and the message "404 Not Found".

