```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static bool IsPrime(int num)
    {
        if (num <= 1)
        {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++)
        {
            if (num % i == 0)
            {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    static int SumOfDigits(int num)
    {
        int sum = 0;

        while (num != 0)
        {
            sum += num % 10;
            num /= 10;
        }

        return sum;
    }

    static string CheckNumber(int num)
    {
        int sum = SumOfDigits(num);

        if (num % sum == 0)
        {
            if (IsPrime(num / sum))
            {
                return "M";
            }

            return "H";
        }

        return "Neither";
    }

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.Write("Enter a number: ");
        int number = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());

        string result = CheckNumber(number);
        Console.WriteLine(result);
    }
}
```

This program defines three methods: `IsPrime`, `SumOfDigits`, and `CheckNumber`.

The `IsPrime` method checks whether a given number is prime or not. It returns true if the number is prime, and false otherwise.

The `SumOfDigits` method calculates the sum of the digits of a given number and returns the result.

The `CheckNumber` method takes a number as input, calculates the sum of its digits using `SumOfDigits`, and checks whether the number is a Harshad number or a Moran number based on the criteria provided. If the number is a Harshad number, it returns "H". If it is a Moran number, it checks whether `num / sum` is prime using `IsPrime` and returns "M" if true. Otherwise, it returns "Neither".

In the `Main` method, the user is prompted to enter a number. The `CheckNumber` method is called with the input number, and the result is printed.

