To handle input with both comma and point as a decimal separator, you can use the `NumberStyles` enumeration along with the `CultureInfo` class in C#. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Globalization;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        while (true)
        {
            Console.Write("Enter a number: ");
            string input = Console.ReadLine();

            // Create a CultureInfo instance that allows both comma and point as decimal separators
            CultureInfo culture = CultureInfo.CreateSpecificCulture("en-US");
            culture.NumberFormat.NumberDecimalSeparator = ".";   // Set decimal separator as point
            culture.NumberFormat.NumberGroupSeparator = ",";     // Set group separator as comma

            double number;
            if (double.TryParse(input, NumberStyles.AllowDecimalPoint | NumberStyles.AllowThousands, culture, out number))
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Valid input. Number: " + number);
                break; // Exit the loop if input is valid
            }
            else
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Invalid input. Please try again.");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we allow both the comma and point as decimal separators by creating a `CultureInfo` instance with the appropriate settings. We set the decimal separator as a point and the group separator as a comma. 

Then, we use `double.TryParse` method with the `NumberStyles.AllowDecimalPoint | NumberStyles.AllowThousands` to parse the input string into a double. This allows us to handle valid numbers with both comma and point as decimal separators.

If the input is valid, we display the number and exit the loop. Otherwise, we display an error message and ask the user to try again.

The `createRouter()` function is typically used to create an instance of the Vue Router in a Vue application. By default, the Vue Router uses the hash mode for routing, which means that the route information is stored in the URL after a hash (#) symbol.

If you want to enable the history mode in the Vue Router, you need to pass the `history` option when calling `createRouter()`.

Here's an example:

```javascript
import { createRouter, createWebHistory } from 'vue-router';

const router = createRouter({
  history: createWebHistory(),
  routes: [
    // Your routes configuration
  ],
});

export default router;
```

In this example, we are importing `createWebHistory` from the `vue-router` package and passing it as the `history` option. This option configures the Vue Router to use the HTML5 History API for routing. With the history mode enabled, the routes will be represented as clean URLs without the hash symbol.

Remember to also update your server configuration to handle the routes correctly when using the history mode.

Sure! Here's the equivalent C++ code for automatically indenting one space every 3 letters in a QLineEdit using the textChanged signal and setCursorPosition:

```cpp
#include <QApplication>
#include <QLineEdit>
#include <QString>

void on_text_changed(QString text) {
    QLineEdit* line_edit = qobject_cast<QLineEdit*>(sender());
    if (!line_edit)
        return;

    int cursor_pos = line_edit->cursorPosition();
    QString new_text;
    for (int i = 0; i < text.length(); i++) {
        new_text += text.at(i);
        if ((i + 1) % 3 == 0)
            new_text += ' ';
    }
    line_edit->setText(new_text);

    cursor_pos += new_text.count(' ', 0, cursor_pos);
    line_edit->setCursorPosition(cursor_pos);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    QApplication app(argc, argv);

    QLineEdit line_edit;
    QObject::connect(&line_edit, &QLineEdit::textChanged, &on_text_changed);
    line_edit.show();

    return app.exec();
}
```

In this code, we define a function `on_text_changed` as a slot that is connected to the `textChanged` signal of the QLineEdit. We use `qobject_cast` to check if the sender of the signal is a QLineEdit pointer. Inside the function, we iterate over each character in the text and add a space after every third character. We then update the line edit's text with the modified text and adjust the cursor position by adding the count of spaces before the current cursor position.

Note that this code assumes you are using the Qt framework in C++. You'll need to include the appropriate Qt header files and use the QApplication class to run the application.

