Here's a possible solution in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public char FirstUniqChar(string s) {
        // Create a dictionary to store the frequency of each character
        Dictionary<char, int> charFrequency = new Dictionary<char, int>();

        // Traverse the string and update the frequency of each character
        foreach(char c in s) {
            if(charFrequency.ContainsKey(c)) {
                charFrequency[c]++;
            } else {
                charFrequency[c] = 1;
            }
        }

        // Traverse the string again and return the first character with frequency 1
        foreach(char c in s) {
            if(charFrequency[c] == 1) {
                return c;
            }
        }

        // If no non-repeating character is found, return '_'
        return '_';
    }
}

public class Program {
    public static void Main(string[] args) {
        // Test the solution with example inputs
        Solution solution = new Solution();
        string input = "leetcode";
        char result = solution.FirstUniqChar(input);
        Console.WriteLine("The first non-repeating character in '" + input + "' is: " + result);
    }
}
```

This solution uses a dictionary to keep track of the frequency of each character in the string. It first traverses the string to calculate the frequency, and then traverses the string again to find the first character with frequency 1. Finally, it returns the found character or '_' if no non-repeating character is found.

