Starting from Vue 3, you can add custom event modifiers using the `.instance` property on the Vue app instance.

Here's an example of adding a custom event modifier called "sticky":

```javascript
const app = createApp(App);

app.config.globalProperties.$eventModifiers = {
  sticky(event) {
    event.stopPropagation();
    // Add your custom logic here
  }
};

app.mount('#app');
```

In the above code, we define a custom event modifier called "sticky" on `$eventModifiers`. The modifier function will receive the original event as a parameter. In this example, we're stopping the propagation of the event using `event.stopPropagation()`, and you can add your custom logic to this function.

After defining the custom event modifier, you can use it in your component's template:

```html
<template>
  <div @click.sticky="handleClick">Click me</div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  methods: {
    handleClick() {
      // Handle the click event
    }
  }
};
</script>
```

In the above component, we use the custom event modifier "sticky" by appending it to the event name. When the "Click me" element is clicked, the "handleClick" method will be called, and any logic defined in the "sticky" event modifier will be executed before the method is called.

Yes, it is possible to create a temporary table with a dynamic query in MS SQL Server. You can use the `EXECUTE` or `sp_executesql` statement to execute dynamic SQL and create the temporary table within it.

Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```sql
-- Define the dynamic query that creates the temporary table
DECLARE @sqlQuery NVARCHAR(MAX) = '
CREATE TABLE #TempTable (
    Id INT,
    Name VARCHAR(50)
);

INSERT INTO #TempTable (Id, Name)
VALUES (1, ''John''), (2, ''Jane''), (3, ''Alice'');
';

-- Execute the dynamic query and create the temporary table
EXECUTE sp_executesql @sqlQuery;

-- Use the temporary table in subsequent statements
SELECT * FROM #TempTable;

-- Remember to drop the temporary table when you're done using it
DROP TABLE #TempTable;
```

In this example, the dynamic query creates a temporary table named `#TempTable` with two columns (`Id` and `Name`). Then, some sample data is inserted into the temporary table, followed by a select statement to retrieve the data. Finally, the temporary table is dropped using the `DROP TABLE` statement.

Remember to be cautious when using dynamic queries due to potential security risks like SQL injection, and always validate and sanitize user input before constructing dynamic SQL queries.

