To add an HTTP header to a SOAP request using Java and the `WebServiceTemplate` class, you can use the `WebServiceMessageCallback` interface along with the `HttpHeaders` class. Here's an example:

```java
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.ws.WebServiceMessage;
import org.springframework.ws.client.core.WebServiceMessageCallback;
import org.springframework.ws.client.core.WebServiceTemplate;
import org.springframework.ws.soap.SoapMessage;

public class SoapRequestWithHttpHeaderExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new WebServiceTemplate instance
        WebServiceTemplate webServiceTemplate = new WebServiceTemplate();

        // Set the default URI for the SOAP service
        webServiceTemplate.setDefaultUri("http://example.com/soap-service");

        // Set the HTTP header values
        HttpHeaders httpHeaders = new HttpHeaders();
        httpHeaders.set("Authorization", "Bearer YourAccessToken");

        // Create a new WebServiceMessageCallback to customize the SOAP request
        WebServiceMessageCallback requestCallback = new WebServiceMessageCallback() {
            @Override
            public void doWithMessage(WebServiceMessage message) {
                // Cast the message to a SoapMessage to manipulate SOAP-specific aspects
                SoapMessage soapMessage = (SoapMessage) message;

                // Set the HTTP headers for the SOAP request
                soapMessage.setSoapAction("http://example.com/soap-service/SomeOperation");
                soapMessage.setHeaders(httpHeaders);
            }
        };

        // Call the SOAP service with the customized request
        webServiceTemplate.sendAndReceive(requestCallback);
    }

}
```

In the above code snippet, we create a `WebServiceTemplate` instance and set its default URI to the SOAP service endpoint. Then, we create an `HttpHeaders` object and set the desired header values, such as the `Authorization` header.

Next, we define a `WebServiceMessageCallback` that overrides the `doWithMessage` method. Inside this method, we cast the `WebServiceMessage` to a `SoapMessage` and set the SOAP action and headers.

Finally, we use the `sendAndReceive` method of the `WebServiceTemplate` to send the SOAP request with the customized HTTP headers.

Note that you will need to replace `http://example.com/soap-service` with the actual URI of your SOAP service, and `http://example.com/soap-service/SomeOperation` with the SOAP action of the specific operation you want to call. Additionally, replace `"Bearer YourAccessToken"` in the `Authorization` header with the actual access token you need to include.

