To delete all values that are equal to 10 in an array, you may use the following steps, depending on the programming language:

1. If the array is mutable (modifiable), you can iterate over all the elements of the array and remove the elements that are equal to 10. 
   - In Python, you can use list comprehension: `arr = [x for x in arr if x != 10]`
   - In JavaScript, you can use the `filter()` method: `arr = arr.filter(x => x !== 10)`
   - In Java, you can use a for loop and ArrayList: 
     ```java
     ArrayList<Integer> newArr = new ArrayList<Integer>();
     for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
         if (arr[i] != 10) {
             newArr.add(arr[i]);
         }
     }
     ```

2. If the array is immutable (not modifiable), you can create a new array or list by excluding the elements equal to 10.
   - In Python, you can use list comprehension: `new_arr = [x for x in arr if x != 10]`
   - In JavaScript, you can use the `filter()` method: `const newArr = arr.filter(x => x !== 10)`
   - In Java, you can create a new array or list and copy the non-10 elements into it:
     ```java
     int[] newArr = new int[arr.length];
     int newIndex = 0;
     for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
         if (arr[i] != 10) {
             newArr[newIndex] = arr[i];
             newIndex++;
         }
     }
     ```

By performing these steps, you will remove all the values equal to 10 from the given array.

