Sure! Here's an example of a simple Hangman game implemented in C#:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string[] words = { "apple", "banana", "cherry", "orange", "strawberry" };
        Random random = new Random();
        string selectedWord = words[random.Next(words.Length)];
        char[] guessedWord = new char[selectedWord.Length];

        for (int i = 0; i < selectedWord.Length; i++)
        {
            guessedWord[i] = '_';
        }

        int attempts = 6;
        bool isGameOver = false;

        Console.WriteLine("Welcome to Hangman!");

        while (!isGameOver)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("\nAttempts left: " + attempts);
            Console.WriteLine("Word: " + string.Join(" ", guessedWord));

            Console.Write("Enter a character: ");
            char input = Console.ReadLine()[0];

            bool isGuessCorrect = false;

            for (int i = 0; i < selectedWord.Length; i++)
            {
                if (selectedWord[i] == input)
                {
                    guessedWord[i] = input;
                    isGuessCorrect = true;
                }
            }

            if (!isGuessCorrect)
            {
                attempts--;
                Console.WriteLine("Incorrect guess!");
            }

            if (attempts == 0)
            {
                isGameOver = true;
                Console.WriteLine("\nYou lose!");
            }

            if (new string(guessedWord) == selectedWord)
            {
                isGameOver = true;
                Console.WriteLine("\nCongratulations! You win!");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this Hangman game, an array of words is defined, a random word is selected, and a char array `guessedWord` is initialized with underscores `_`. The player has 6 attempts to guess the word correctly.

The game is played in a loop until the player wins or runs out of attempts. In each iteration, the current status of the game is displayed, the player enters a character to guess, and the input is checked against the selected word. If the guess is correct, the guessed word array is updated to reveal the correctly guessed characters. If the guess is incorrect, the attempts are decreased, and an appropriate message is displayed.

At the end of each iteration, the game checks if the player has won by comparing the `guessedWord` with the selected word. If the player wins, a victory message is displayed, and if the attempts reach 0, a failure message is displayed.

