Here is a program in Java that reverses a given string:

```java
public class StringReverse {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "Hello, World!";
        String reversed = reverseString(input);
        System.out.println(reversed);
    }

    public static String reverseString(String input) {
        StringBuilder reversed = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = input.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            reversed.append(input.charAt(i));
        }

        return reversed.toString();
    }
}
```

In this program, we are using a `for` loop to iterate through the characters of the input string in reverse order. The loop runs from `i = input.length() - 1` to `i >= 0`. The assignment operation `reversed.append(input.charAt(i))` is executed in each iteration of the loop to build the reversed string.

In terms of execution, the loop will execute `input.length()` times since we are iterating through the input string character by character. The assignment operation `reversed.append(input.charAt(i))` will also execute `input.length()` times as it is called once in each iteration of the loop.

For example, if the input string is `"Hello, World!"`, the loop and the assignment operation will execute 13 times as the input string has 13 characters.

Using the Insertion Sort algorithm, here are the intermediate steps to sort the given list `[25, 15, 5, 10, 12, 7, 9, 18]`:

Step 1: [25, 15, 5, 10, 12, 7, 9, 18]
Step 2: [15, 25, 5, 10, 12, 7, 9, 18] (Swapped 25 and 15)
Step 3: [5, 15, 25, 10, 12, 7, 9, 18] (Swapped 25 and 5)
Step 4: [5, 10, 15, 25, 12, 7, 9, 18]
Step 5: [5, 10, 12, 15, 25, 7, 9, 18]
Step 6: [5, 7, 10, 12, 15, 25, 9, 18] (Swapped 25 and 7)
Step 7: [5, 7, 9, 10, 12, 15, 25, 18] (Swapped 25 and 9)
Step 8: [5, 7, 9, 10, 12, 15, 18, 25] (Swapped 25 and 18)

To count the number of swap operations, we can modify the Insertion Sort algorithm to include a counter that increments each time a swap is performed. Here's the modified code:

```java
public class InsertionSort {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {25, 15, 5, 10, 12, 7, 9, 18};
        insertionSort(arr);
        System.out.println("Sorted Array: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
    }

    public static void insertionSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int swapCount = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            int key = arr[i];
            int j = i - 1;

            while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
                arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
                j--;
                swapCount++;
            }

            arr[j + 1] = key;
        }

        System.out.println("Total Swap Operations: " + swapCount);
    }
}
```

By running this modified code, you will find that the loop executes a total of 7 swap operations to sort the given list.

