In Handlebars, variables are usually passed as properties of an object when compiling a template. These variables are often referred to as "context" or "data".

If you want to use private variables within a Handlebars template, you can achieve this by organizing your data object in a way that includes both public and private variables. Here's an example:

```javascript
const Handlebars = require('handlebars');

// Private variable
const privateVariable = 'This is private';

// Data object
const data = {
  publicVariable: 'This is public',
  privateVariable: privateVariable // Include the private variable in the data object
};

// Create a Handlebars template
const template = Handlebars.compile('Public: {{publicVariable}}, Private: {{privateVariable}}');

// Compile the template with the data object
const output = template(data);

console.log(output);
```

In this example, the `privateVariable` is defined outside the template and added to the `data` object. When the template is compiled using `Handlebars.compile()`, the private variable can be accessed within the template using `{{privateVariable}}`.

The output of the above code would be: "Public: This is public, Private: This is private".

By following this approach, you can effectively use private variables within Handlebars templates. Remember to appropriately handle the scope and visibility of your private variables as per your application's requirements.

The "runtime options data" in Handlebars refers to the extra data that can be passed to the template during runtime. These options are helpful when you need to dynamically change the behavior or output of the template based on certain conditions.

To use the runtime options data in Handlebars, you can make use of the `options.fn` and `options.data` functions inside your template. Here's an example:

```javascript
const Handlebars = require('handlebars');

// Create a Handlebars template
const template = Handlebars.compile('{{#if showInfo}}{{name}} - {{options.fn this}}{{/if}}');

// Data object
const data = {
  name: 'John Doe',
  showInfo: true
};

// Runtime options data
const runtimeOptions = {
  data: {
    additionalInfo: 'Additional information'
  }
};

// Compile the template with the data object and runtime options
const output = template(data, runtimeOptions);

console.log(output);
```

In the above example, the template checks the `showInfo` property of the data object. If it evaluates to true, it renders the name followed by the additional information from the runtime options.

By passing the runtime options as the second argument when invoking the template function, you can access these options using `options.fn` inside the template. The `options.data` function can be used to access additional data properties.

The output of the above code would be: "John Doe - Additional information".

Feel free to adapt the example to suit your specific needs and integrate the runtime options data into your Handlebars templates.

