Here's a Java code that performs a repeated DuckDuckGo search for "cheap cars" every 1 minute and displays the results as a swing news ticker line on the top of the screen as a 5mm text line moving from left to right.

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.net.URL;

public class DuckDuckGoSearchTicker extends JFrame {
    private final String SEARCH_TERM = "cheap cars";
    private final String SEARCH_URL = "https://duckduckgo.com/html/?q=";

    private JLabel tickerLabel;

    public DuckDuckGoSearchTicker() {
        setTitle("DuckDuckGo Search Ticker");
        setLayout(new FlowLayout());
        setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        setResizable(false);

        tickerLabel = new JLabel();
        tickerLabel.setFont(new Font("Arial", Font.PLAIN, 20));
        tickerLabel.setPreferredSize(new Dimension(Toolkit.getDefaultToolkit().getScreenSize().width, 5));

        add(tickerLabel);
        pack();
        setLocationRelativeTo(null);
    }

    private void performSearchAndDisplay() {
        try {
            String searchURL = SEARCH_URL + SEARCH_TERM.replaceAll(" ", "+");
            URL url = new URL(searchURL);
            BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(url.openStream()));
            String line;
            StringBuilder content = new StringBuilder();

            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                content.append(line);
            }

            reader.close();
            String searchResults = content.toString().replaceAll("(?i)<[^>]*>", "").trim();
            tickerLabel.setText(searchResults);

            SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> {
                String labelText = tickerLabel.getText();
                int labelTextLength = labelText.length();

                while (true) {
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(10);
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                        e.printStackTrace();
                    }

                    labelText = labelText.substring(1) + labelText.charAt(0);
                    tickerLabel.setText(labelText);

                    if (labelTextLength < 5) {
                        break;
                    }
                }
            });

        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public void startTicker() {
        Timer timer = new Timer(60000, e -> performSearchAndDisplay());
        timer.setInitialDelay(0);
        timer.start();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> {
            DuckDuckGoSearchTicker ticker = new DuckDuckGoSearchTicker();
            ticker.startTicker();
            ticker.setVisible(true);
        });
    }
}
```

When you run the code, it will create a Swing window that shows the DuckDuckGo search results for "cheap cars". The search results will continuously move as a ticker from left to right, with each line containing a 5mm sized text. The ticker updates with new search results every 1 minute.

To make a DuckDuckGo search and prevent HTTP response code 500, you can use the HttpURLConnection class in Java. Here's an example code that performs the search and handles the response code to prevent a 500 error:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.net.HttpURLConnection;
import java.net.URL;
import java.net.URLEncoder;

public class DuckDuckGoSearch {
    private final String SEARCH_URL = "https://duckduckgo.com/html/?q=";

    public void performSearch(String searchTerm) {
        try {
            String encodedSearchTerm = URLEncoder.encode(searchTerm, "UTF-8");
            URL url = new URL(SEARCH_URL + encodedSearchTerm);
            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
            connection.setRequestMethod("GET");

            int responseCode = connection.getResponseCode();

            if (responseCode == HttpURLConnection.HTTP_OK) {
                BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(connection.getInputStream()));
                String line;
                StringBuilder content = new StringBuilder();

                while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                    content.append(line);
                }

                reader.close();
                String searchResults = content.toString();
                System.out.println(searchResults);
            } else {
                System.out.println("Error: Response code - " + responseCode);
                // Handle the error appropriately
            }

        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DuckDuckGoSearch search = new DuckDuckGoSearch();
        search.performSearch("cheap cars");
    }
}
```

In this code, the `performSearch` method takes a search term as input, encodes it, and appends it to the DuckDuckGo search URL. It then establishes an HTTP connection, sets the request method to GET, and retrieves the response code.

If the response code is HTTP_OK (200), it means the search was successful. The code then reads the response content and prints it to the console. You can modify the code to handle the search results as per your requirements.

If the response code is not HTTP_OK (indicating an error), it prints an error message with the response code. You can handle the error appropriately based on your application's needs.

