Here's an example code to create an Android app in React Native that checks for VPN permissions and asks the user for permission on startup:

1. First, create a new React Native project using the following command:

```bash
npx react-native init VPNApp
```

2. Navigate to the project directory:

```bash
cd VPNApp
```

3. Open the `App.js` file and replace the content with the following code:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text, Button, PermissionsAndroid } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const [vpnPermissionGranted, setVpnPermissionGranted] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    checkVPNPermission();
  }, []);

  const checkVPNPermission = async () => {
    try {
      const granted = await PermissionsAndroid.check(
        PermissionsAndroid.PERMISSIONS.ACCESS_VPN
      );
      if (granted === PermissionsAndroid.RESULTS.GRANTED) {
        setVpnPermissionGranted(true);
      } else {
        requestVPNPermission();
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.warn('Error checking VPN permission:', error);
    }
  };

  const requestVPNPermission = async () => {
    try {
      const granted = await PermissionsAndroid.request(
        PermissionsAndroid.PERMISSIONS.ACCESS_VPN
      );
      if (granted === PermissionsAndroid.RESULTS.GRANTED) {
        setVpnPermissionGranted(true);
      } else {
        setVpnPermissionGranted(false);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.warn('Error requesting VPN permission:', error);
    }
  };

  return (
    <View style={{ flex: 1, alignItems: 'center', justifyContent: 'center' }}>
      <Text style={{ fontSize: 20, marginBottom: 20 }}>
        VPN Permission Status:
      </Text>
      <Text style={{ fontSize: 16, marginBottom: 30 }}>
        {vpnPermissionGranted ? 'Granted' : 'Not Granted'}
      </Text>
      {!vpnPermissionGranted && (
        <Button title="Request VPN Permission" onPress={requestVPNPermission} />
      )}
    </View>
  );
};

export default App;
```

4. Open a terminal and start the Metro server with the following command:

```bash
npx react-native start
```

5. Open another terminal and run the app on the Android emulator or device using the command:

```bash
npx react-native run-android
```

The app will check for VPN permissions on startup. If the permission is granted, it will display "Granted" in the app. Otherwise, it will show "Not Granted" and provide a button to request the VPN permission from the user.

