   glStencilOp(GL_REPLACE, GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP);

   // Set the stencil comparison function to always pass
   glStencilFunc(GL_ALWAYS, 1, 0xFF);

   // Set the stencil mask to 0xFF to write all bits
   glStencilMask(0xFF);
   ```

3. Render the geometry that covers the subset of pixels you want to modify. The stencil buffer will be updated with the reference value (in this case, 1) where the geometry is rendered.

4. Set up a different stencil test configuration that only allows rendering where the stencil value matches the reference value.
   ```cpp
   // Set the stencil operation to keep the existing stencil value
   glStencilOp(GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP);

   // Set the stencil comparison function to only pass where the stencil value matches the reference value
   glStencilFunc(GL_EQUAL, 1, 0xFF);

   // Disable writing to the stencil buffer
   glStencilMask(0x00);
   ```

5. Use the updated stencil test configuration and modify the color of the corresponding fragments in the fragment shader. Be careful to only modify the color of the pixels that have passed the stencil test.
   ```glsl
   void main() {
       if (gl_FragCoord.x <= uSubsetMaxX && gl_FragCoord.x >= uSubsetMinX &&
           gl_FragCoord.y <= uSubsetMaxY && gl_FragCoord.y >= uSubsetMinY) {
           vec4 color = texture2D(uTexture, vTexCoord);
           // ...
           color = vec4(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0); // example modification: set color to red
           // ...
           gl_FragColor = color;
       } else {
           // No modification required, output the original color
           gl_FragColor = texture2D(uTexture, vTexCoord);
       }
   }
   ```

6. Finally, disable the stencil test when you no longer need it.
   ```cpp
