   ```

By using a separate texture object to store the stencil buffer, you can efficiently keep statically known subsets of pixels and reuse them as a stencil across multiple frames. This approach avoids setting or updating the stencil buffer one by one every frame, leading to improved efficiency in rendering and modification.

To efficiently calculate the union of multiple stencil textures without having all the possible unions precalculated as textures, you can use a shader-based approach that leverages bitwise operations.

Here's a step-by-step explanation of how you can efficiently calculate the union of multiple stencil textures using GLSL:

Initialization:
1. Create multiple stencil textures, each representing a different subset using the same process mentioned earlier for creating a stencil texture.

Subsequent frames:
1. Enable the stencil test and bind the first stencil texture before rendering any geometry.
   ```cpp
   glEnable(GL_STENCIL_TEST);
   glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, firstStencilTexture);
   ```

2. Set up the stencil test configuration for the first stencil texture.
   ```cpp
   // Set the stencil operation to replace the stencil value with the reference value
   glStencilOp(GL_REPLACE, GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP);

   // Set the stencil comparison function to always pass
   glStencilFunc(GL_ALWAYS, 1, 0xFF);

   // Set the stencil mask to 0xFF to write all bits
   glStencilMask(0xFF);
   ```

3. Render the geometry that covers the first subset of pixels you want to modify. The stencil buffer for the first stencil texture will be updated with the reference value (in this case, 1) where the geometry is rendered.

4. Repeat the process for the remaining stencil textures, binding and setting up the stencil test configuration for each one, and rendering the corresponding geometry.

5. Set up a final stencil test configuration that performs a bitwise OR operation on the stencil values of all the textures to calculate the union.
   ```cpp
   // Set the stencil operation to keep the existing stencil value
   glStencilOp(GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP);

   // Set the stencil comparison function to only pass where the stencil value is non-zero
   glStencilFunc(GL_NOTEQUAL, 0, 0xFF);

   // Disable writing to the stencil buffer
   glStencilMask(0x00);
   ```

6. Use the updated stencil test configuration and modify the color of the corresponding fragments in the fragment shader. Only fragments that have passed the stencil test will be modified.
   ```glsl
   void main() {
       if (gl_FragCoord.x <= uSubsetMaxX && gl_FragCoord.x >= uSubsetMinX &&
           gl_FragCoord.y <= uSubsetMaxY && gl_FragCoord.y >= uSubsetMinY) {
           vec4 color = texture2D(uTexture, vTexCoord);
           // ...
           color = vec4(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0); // example modification: set color to red
           // ...
           gl_FragColor = color;
       } else {
           // No modification required, output the original color
           gl_FragColor = texture2D(uTexture, vTexCoord);
       }
   }
   ```

7. Finally, disable the stencil test when you no longer need it.
   ```cpp
   glDisable(GL_STENCIL_TEST);
   ```

By using bitmask operations in the shader, you can efficiently calculate the union of multiple stencil textures without explicitly storing all the possible unions. This approach avoids the need to precalculate and manage a large number of combinations, resulting in improved efficiency in calculating the stencil union. Remember that you can combine different bitwise operations (AND, OR, XOR) based on your specific requirements for combining the stencil textures.

To build a landing page with a full name, phone number, and gender using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, you can follow the steps below:

1. Set up the basic HTML structure:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Landing Page</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Sign up</h1>
    <form id="signup-form">
      <label for="full-name">Full Name</label>
      <input type="text" id="full-name" required>

      <label for="phone-number">Phone Number</label>
      <input type="text" id="phone-number" required>

      <label for="gender">Gender</label>
      <select id="gender" required>
        <option value="">Select gender</option>
        <option value="male">Male</option>
        <option value="female">Female</option>
        <option value="other">Other</option>
      </select>

      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  </div>

  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a CSS file (style.css) to style the landing page:

```css
.container {
  max-width: 480px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 40px;
}

h1 {
  text-align: center;
}

form label {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 20px;
}

form input, form select {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px;
  margin-top: 5px;
}

form button {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px;
  margin-top: 20px;
  background-color: #4CAF50;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
}

form button:hover {
  background-color: #45a049;
}
```

3. Add JavaScript functionality to handle form submission (script.js):

```javascript
document.getElementById('signup-form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  event.preventDefault(); // Prevent form submission

  // Get form field values
  var fullName = document.getElementById('full-name').value;
  var phoneNumber = document.getElementById('phone-number').value;
  var gender = document.getElementById('gender').value;

  // Print the values to the console for testing
  console.log('Full Name: ' + fullName);
  console.log('Phone Number: ' + phoneNumber);
  console.log('Gender: ' + gender);

  // Clear form fields
  document.getElementById('full-name').value = '';
  document.getElementById('phone-number').value = '';
  document.getElementById('gender').value = '';

  // Show success message or perform additional actions
});
```

That's it! You now have a basic landing page with a form for users to enter their full name, phone number, and gender. When the form is submitted, the JavaScript code will retrieve the field values and display them in the console. You can modify the JavaScript code to perform other actions such as displaying a success message or sending the form data to a server.

