In Next.js, you can use the `session` package to save data in a session.

First, install the `next-iron-session` package:

```bash
npm install next-iron-session
```

or

```bash
yarn add next-iron-session
```

Next, create a `pages/api` directory in your Next.js project and create a new file called `session.js`. This file will handle creating a session and saving data to it.

Inside `session.js`, add the following code:

```javascript
import { withIronSession } from "next-iron-session";

export default function withSession(handler) {
  return withIronSession(handler, {
    password: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,
    cookieName: "<YOUR_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME>",
    cookieOptions: {
      secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production", // set this to false in development
    },
  });
}
```

Replace `<YOUR_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME>` with the desired name for your session cookie.

Now, you can use this session middleware in your API routes to save data.

For example, create a new file in the `pages/api` directory called `save-data.js`. In this file, add the following code:

```javascript
import withSession from "./session";

export default withSession(async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { data } = req.body; // Retrieve data from request body

    req.session.set("data", data); // Save data to session
    await req.session.save(); // Save session

    res.status(200).json({ message: "Data saved successfully" });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: "An error occurred" });
  }
});
```

In this example, the `save-data` endpoint receives data in the request body and saves it to the session.

To access the saved data in another API route or page, import the `withSession` middleware and use `req.session.get("data")` to retrieve the stored value.

Note: It's important to secure your session by setting a unique `SESSION_SECRET` environment variable in your project.

