In Next.js, you can use the `next-iron-session` package to implement session-based data storage. Here's how you can use it:

1. Install the `next-iron-session` package:

```bash
npm install next-iron-session
```

or

```bash
yarn add next-iron-session
```

2. Create a `pages/api` directory in your Next.js project if it doesn't already exist.

3. Inside the `pages/api` directory, create a file called `session.js`. In this file, add the following code:

```javascript
import { withIronSession } from "next-iron-session";

const sessionHandler = withIronSession(
  async (req, res) => {
    const { method } = req;

    if (method === "GET") {
      // Retrieve data from session
      const data = req.session.get("data");
      return res.status(200).json({ data });
    } else if (method === "POST") {
      // Save data to session
      const { data } = req.body;
      req.session.set("data", data);
      await req.session.save();
      return res.status(200).json({ message: "Data saved successfully" });
    } else {
      return res.status(405).json({ message: "Method Not Allowed" });
    }
  },
  {
    password: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,
    cookieName: "<YOUR_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME>",
    cookieOptions: {
      secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
    },
  }
);

export default sessionHandler;
```

Replace `<YOUR_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME>` with the desired name for your session cookie. Also, make sure to set the `SESSION_SECRET` environment variable with a secure value.

4. Create another file in the `pages/api` directory called `save-data.js`. In this file, add the following code:

```javascript
import sessionHandler from "./session";

export default sessionHandler;
```

5. In any component or page, you can use HTTP methods to save and retrieve data from the session. For example, create a file called `MyPage.js` in the `pages` directory:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";
import axios from "axios";

const MyPage = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState("");

  const handleSaveData = async () => {
    try {
      await axios.post("/api/save-data", { data: "Hello, Next.js!" });
      alert("Data saved successfully");
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      alert("An error occurred");
    }
  };

  const handleRetrieveData = async () => {
    try {
      const response = await axios.get("/api/save-data");
      setData(response.data.data);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      alert("An error occurred");
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleSaveData}>Save Data</button>
      <button onClick={handleRetrieveData}>Retrieve Data</button>
      <p>{data}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyPage;
```

In this example, the `handleSaveData` function sends a POST request to the `save-data` API route to save the data to the session. The `handleRetrieveData` function sends a GET request to retrieve the data from the session.

By integrating the above code, you can save and retrieve data using session storage in Next.js.

To share data between JavaScript scripts in Next.js, you can make use of global variables, module exports/imports, or React's centralized state management. Here's how you can achieve each of these methods:

1. Global Variables:
   Create a global variable in a script file and access it from other scripts.

   ```javascript
   // In script1.js
   window.sharedData = { value: "Hello, Next.js!" };

   // In script2.js
   const sharedData = window.sharedData;
   console.log(sharedData.value); // "Hello, Next.js!"
   ```

   By assigning the data to the `window` object, it becomes accessible across script files.

2. Module Exports/Imports:
   Create a separate JavaScript file and export the desired data. Import that file in other script files to access the exported data.

   ```javascript
   // In sharedData.js
   export const sharedData = { value: "Hello, Next.js!" };

   // In script.js
   import { sharedData } from "./sharedData.js";
   console.log(sharedData.value); // "Hello, Next.js!"
   ```

   Using `export` and `import`, you can share data between different JavaScript modules.

3. React's Centralized State Management:
   If you are using React in your Next.js application, you can leverage React's state management libraries like Redux, MobX, or the built-in `useState` and `useContext` hooks to manage and share data between components.

   ```javascript
   // In sharedDataContext.js
   import React, { createContext, useState } from "react";

   export const SharedDataContext = createContext();

   export const SharedDataProvider = ({ children }) => {
     const [sharedData, setSharedData] = useState({ value: "Hello, Next.js!" });

     return (
       <SharedDataContext.Provider value={{ sharedData, setSharedData }}>
         {children}
       </SharedDataContext.Provider>
     );
   };

   // In component1.js
   import React, { useContext } from "react";
   import { SharedDataContext } from "./sharedDataContext";

   const Component1 = () => {
     const { sharedData } = useContext(SharedDataContext);
     console.log(sharedData.value); // "Hello, Next.js!"

     return <div>Component 1</div>;
   };

   // In component2.js
   import React, { useContext } from "react";
   import { SharedDataContext } from "./sharedDataContext";

   const Component2 = () => {
     const { sharedData, setSharedData } = useContext(SharedDataContext);

     const updateValue = () => {
       setSharedData({ value: "Updated value" });
     };

     return (
       <div>
         <p>{sharedData.value}</p>
         <button onClick={updateValue}>Update Value</button>
       </div>
     );
   };
   ```

   By creating a centralized state using `createContext` and `useState`, you can provide the state value and updating functions to multiple components with the help of the `useContext` hook.

Choose the method that best suits your use case based on the complexity and requirements of your data sharing scenario.

