#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

int randrange(int a, int b) {
    // Ensure a is smaller than or equal to b
    if (a > b) {
        int temp = a;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }

    // Calculate the range size
    int rangeSize = b - a + 1;

    // Generate a random integer within the range
    int randomNum = rand() % rangeSize + a;

    return randomNum;
}

int main() {
    // Seed the random number generator
    srand(time(0));

    // Generate and print 10 random numbers within the range [5, 15]
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        int num = randrange(5, 15);
        printf("%d\n", num);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this implementation, the randrange function first ensures that a is smaller than or equal to b by swapping them if necessary. It then calculates the size of the range by subtracting a from b and adding 1. Finally, it uses the rand function to generate a random integer within the rangeSize and adds it to a to get the final random number.

The main function demonstrates the usage of the randrange function by generating and printing 10 random numbers within the range [5, 15].

Apologies for the oversight. To ensure a uniformly random distribution regardless of the range size, you can use the rejection sampling technique. Here's an updated implementation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <limits.h>

int randrange(int a, int b) {
    // Ensure a is smaller than or equal to b
    if (a > b) {
        int temp = a;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }

    // Calculate the range size
    long long rangeSize = (long long)b - a + 1;

    // Find the maximum number that can be generated by rand function
    long long maxRand = (long long)RAND_MAX + 1;

    // Calculate the number of equal-sized ranges within maxRand
    long long numRanges = maxRand / rangeSize;

    // Calculate the maximum valid range (multiple of rangeSize)
    long long maxValidRange = numRanges * rangeSize;

    // Generate a random number within the valid range using rejection sampling
    long long randomNum;
    do {
        randomNum = rand();
    } while (randomNum >= maxValidRange);

    // Map the random number to the desired range and return it
    int mappedNum = randomNum % rangeSize + a;

    return mappedNum;
}

int main() {
    // Seed the random number generator
    srand(time(0));

    // Generate and print 10 random numbers within the range [5, 15]
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        int num = randrange(5, 15);
        printf("%d\n", num);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this updated implementation, we calculate the maximum number that can be generated by the rand function (maxRand). Then, we divide maxRand by the rangeSize to determine the number of equal-sized ranges (numRanges). We only accept random numbers that are less than the maximum valid range (maxValidRange). If we encounter a number outside the valid range, we generate another random number. After obtaining a valid random number, we map it to the desired range using the modulo operator and return the result. This ensures a uniformly random distribution within the given range, regardless of the range size.

