Yes, in JavaScript, `enumerable` is a property attribute that determines whether a property can be enumerated using a `for..in` loop or the `Object.keys()` method.

When defining an object property using the `Object.defineProperty()` method or using object literals, you can specify the `enumerable` attribute as `true` or `false`:

```javascript
Object.defineProperty(object, property, {
  enumerable: true|false
});
```

If `enumerable` is set to `true`, the property can be enumerated. If it is set to `false`, the property cannot be enumerated.

By default, if the `enumerable` attribute is not specified during property creation, it is set to `true`.

Here's an example:

```javascript
var person = {
  name: "John",
  age: 30
};

Object.defineProperty(person, "country", {
  value: "USA",
  enumerable: false
});

console.log(Object.keys(person)); // Output: ["name", "age"]
```

In the above example, the property `country` is not enumerable because its `enumerable` attribute is set to `false`. When we call `Object.keys(person)`, it returns an array of the enumerable property names of the `person` object, which is `["name", "age"]` in this case, since the `country` property is not included.

A non-enumerable property in JavaScript is a property that cannot be iterated over using a `for..in` loop or accessed through the `Object.keys()` or `Object.getOwnPropertyNames()` methods. Non-enumerable properties are excluded from these operations.

When an object property is defined as non-enumerable, it is not considered when iterating over the object's properties or extracting the keys of the object. This can be useful when you want to hide certain properties from being enumerated or when you want to prevent accidental modification of specific properties.

Non-enumerable properties can be created using the `Object.defineProperty()` method and setting the `enumerable` attribute to `false`. For example:

```javascript
var obj = {};

Object.defineProperty(obj, "nonEnumerableProp", {
  value: "This is a non-enumerable property",
  enumerable: false
});

for (var prop in obj) {
  console.log(prop); // This will not include "nonEnumerableProp"
}

console.log(Object.keys(obj)); // This will not include "nonEnumerableProp"
```

In the above example, the property `nonEnumerableProp` is defined as non-enumerable. When we try to iterate through the properties of the `obj` object using a `for..in` loop, or extract the keys using `Object.keys()`, the `nonEnumerableProp` will not be included in the output.

