Here's the solution in C# to convert a duodecimal number to decimal and vice versa:

```csharp
using System;

public class BaseConverter
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string duodecimalInput = Console.ReadLine();
        int decimalResult = DuodecimalToDecimal(duodecimalInput);
        Console.WriteLine(decimalResult);

        int decimalInput = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
        string duodecimalResult = DecimalToDuodecimal(decimalInput);
        Console.WriteLine(duodecimalResult);
    }

    public static int DuodecimalToDecimal(string duodecimal)
    {
        int decimalNumber = 0;
        int power = 0;

        for (int i = duodecimal.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            char digit = duodecimal[i];
            int decimalValue;

            if (digit >= '0' && digit <= '9')
            {
                decimalValue = digit - '0';
            }
            else if (digit >= 'A' && digit <= 'B')
            {
                decimalValue = digit - 'A' + 10;
            }
            else
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Invalid duodecimal digit: " + digit);
            }

            decimalNumber += decimalValue * (int) Math.Pow(12, power);
            power++;
        }

        return decimalNumber;
    }

    public static string DecimalToDuodecimal(int decimalNumber)
    {
        string duodecimal = "";

        while (decimalNumber > 0)
        {
            int remainder = decimalNumber % 12;

            if (remainder < 10)
            {
                duodecimal = remainder + duodecimal;
            }
            else
            {
                duodecimal = (char)('A' + remainder - 10) + duodecimal;
            }

            decimalNumber /= 12;
        }

        if (duodecimal == "")
        {
            duodecimal = "0";
        }

        return "0w" + duodecimal;
    }
}
```

The `DuodecimalToDecimal` method takes a duodecimal number as input and converts it to a decimal number using the positional value system. The method iterates through each digit of the duodecimal number from right to left, and for each digit, it calculates its decimal value based on its position (using powers of 12) and adds it to the result.

The `DecimalToDuodecimal` method takes a decimal number as input and converts it to a duodecimal number by repeatedly dividing the decimal number by 12 and keeping track of the remainders. If the remainder is less than 10, it is appended as a digit to the left of the existing duodecimal representation. If the remainder is 10 or greater, the corresponding duodecimal digit ('A' for 10, 'B' for 11) is appended to the left. The resulting duodecimal string is prefixed with "0w" to represent that it is in the duodecimal base.

In the `Main` method, we read the input: the duodecimal number `n` and the decimal number `m`. We call the respective conversion methods and print the results.

