The error message "Invalid options in vue.config.js: 'server' is not allowed" typically occurs when you have an invalid or unsupported configuration option in your `vue.config.js` file.

The `vue.config.js` file is used to customize the configuration of the Vue CLI when serving or building your Vue.js application.

The error specifically mentions the "server" option, which likely means you have added or specified a "server" option in your `vue.config.js` file that is not recognized by the Vue CLI.

To fix this error, you should check your `vue.config.js` file and make sure you are not using any unsupported or invalid options. Remove or correct any instances of the "server" option.

Here's an example of a valid `vue.config.js` file:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  // Configuration options...
}
```

If you need to configure the server settings for your application, you can use the `devServer` option instead:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  devServer: {
    // Configuration options...
  }
}
```

Make sure to consult the Vue CLI documentation for a complete list of supported configuration options: [Vue CLI Configuration Reference](https://cli.vuejs.org/config/).

