To retrieve the value from a selected checkbox in Angular and send it to a function, you can do the following:

1. Create a checkbox in your HTML template using the `ngModel` directive to bind the checkbox to a variable in your component:
```html
<input type="checkbox" [(ngModel)]="checkboxValue" (change)="sendValueToFunction(checkboxValue)">
```

2. In your component class, define the `checkboxValue` variable to hold the value of the checkbox:
```javascript
export class YourComponent {
  checkboxValue: boolean;

  sendValueToFunction(value: boolean) {
    // Call your function with the checkbox value
    console.log(value);
    // or do something else with the value
  }
}
```

In this example, the `ngModel` directive binds the checkbox input value to the `checkboxValue` variable in the component. The `(change)` event is triggered whenever the checkbox state changes, invoking the `sendValueToFunction()` method with the current checkbox value as an argument.

If you have multiple checkboxes returned from an array in TypeScript and you want to retrieve the values of the selected checkboxes and send them to a function, you can use the following approach:

1. In your component class, define an array to hold the checkboxes' values and create a method to handle the selected checkboxes:

```typescript
export class YourComponent {
  checkboxValues: any[] = ['value1', 'value2', 'value3', 'value4'];

  handleCheckboxChange(value: any) {
    // Do something with the selected checkbox value
    console.log(value);
    // or call your function with the selected checkbox value
    this.sendValueToFunction(value);
  }

  sendValueToFunction(value: any) {
    // Call your function with the selected checkbox value
    console.log(value);
    // or do something else with the value
  }
}
```

2. In your HTML template, use the `*ngFor` directive to iterate over the checkbox values array and bind each checkbox to its corresponding value:

```html
<div *ngFor="let value of checkboxValues">
  <input type="checkbox" [(ngModel)]="value.selected" (change)="handleCheckboxChange(value)">
  <label>{{ value }}</label>
</div>
```

In this example, we use the `*ngFor` directive to iterate over the `checkboxValues` array. For each value in the array, a checkbox is created and bound to the `selected` property of the value object using `[(ngModel)]`. The `(change)` event is triggered whenever a checkbox state changes, passing the value object to the `handleCheckboxChange()` method. Inside the `handleCheckboxChange()` method, you can access the selected checkbox value and pass it to the `sendValueToFunction()` method or perform any other desired actions.

If you want the function to be triggered when a button is clicked instead of when a checkbox is changed, you can modify the previous code as follows:

1. In your component class, create a new method that will be called when the button is clicked. Inside this method, you can loop through the checkbox values array and check if each checkbox is selected. If it is selected, you can send its value to the desired function.

```typescript
export class YourComponent {
  checkboxValues: any[] = ['value1', 'value2', 'value3', 'value4'];

  sendSelectedValues() {
    const selectedValues = this.checkboxValues.filter(value => value.selected);
    selectedValues.forEach(value => this.sendValueToFunction(value));
  }

  sendValueToFunction(value: any) {
    // Call your function with the selected checkbox value
    console.log(value);
    // or do something else with the value
  }
}
```

2. In your HTML template, add a button and bind it to the `sendSelectedValues()` method using the `(click)` event.

```html
<button (click)="sendSelectedValues()">Send Selected Values</button>
```

Now, when the button is clicked, the `sendSelectedValues()` method will be invoked. It will iterate through the `checkboxValues` array, filter the selected checkboxes, and call the `sendValueToFunction()` method for each selected value.

