To get the JSON response from an API, you can use the `json` package in Go to decode the response body.

Here's an example of how you can modify your code to get the JSON response:

```go
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"net/http"
)

type UserAgent struct {
	ID     int    `json:"id"`
	Agent  string `json:"user_agent"`
	Source string `json:"source"`
}

func getUserAgents() {
	url := "http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/get_ua"

	resp, err := http.Get(url)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()

	var userAgents []UserAgent

	err = json.NewDecoder(resp.Body).Decode(&userAgents)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	for _, ua := range userAgents {
		fmt.Printf("ID: %d\nAgent: %s\nSource: %s\n\n", ua.ID, ua.Agent, ua.Source)
	}
}

func main() {
	getUserAgents()
}
```

In this example, I assume that the JSON response from the API contains an array of user agents with fields `id`, `user_agent`, and `source`. You can customize the `UserAgent` struct to match the structure of your JSON response.

The `json.NewDecoder(resp.Body).Decode(&userAgents)` line decodes the JSON response from the `resp.Body` into the `userAgents` slice.

Finally, you can iterate over the `userAgents` slice to access the individual user agents and their fields.

