import java.lang.annotation.Annotation;
import javassist.ClassPool;
import javassist.CtClass;
import javassist.CtMethod;

public class MethodCopyExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Original method
            ClassPool pool = ClassPool.getDefault();
            CtClass originalClass = pool.get("YourClass"); // Replace "YourClass" with your class's fully qualified name
            CtMethod originalMethod = originalClass.getDeclaredMethod("yourMethod"); // Replace "yourMethod" with your method's name

            // Create a new method in the same class
            CtMethod newMethod = CtMethod.copy(originalMethod, originalClass, null);

            // Copy the annotations from the original method to the new method
            Object[] annotations = originalMethod.getAnnotations();
            for (Object annotation : annotations) {
                newMethod.getMethodInfo().addAnnotation((Annotation) annotation);
            }

            // Do any modifications to the copied method if required

            // Save the modified class
            originalClass.writeFile();
            System.out.println("Method copied successfully!");

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace "YourClass" with the fully qualified name of your class, and "yourMethod" with the name of your method that you want to copy.

This code retrieves the original method using `ClassPool` and `CtClass`. Then, it creates a new method using `CtMethod.copy()` by passing the original method and the same class. Next, it copies all the original method's annotations to the new method using `getAnnotations()` and `addAnnotation()`. You can modify the copied method as per your requirements, and finally, save the modified class using `writeFile()`.

To copy a Java method with its annotations in Java, you can use reflection to access and replicate the method along with its associated annotations. Here is an example:

```java
import java.lang.annotation.Annotation;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;

class ExampleClass {
    @Override
    @CustomAnnotation("Example annotation")
    public void exampleMethod() {
        // Method implementation
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws NoSuchMethodException {
        // Get the original method and its annotations
        Method originalMethod = ExampleClass.class.getMethod("exampleMethod");
        Annotation[] annotations = originalMethod.getAnnotations();

        // Create a new method with the same name and parameters
        Method copiedMethod = ExampleClass.class.getDeclaredMethod(
                originalMethod.getName(), originalMethod.getParameterTypes());

        // Set the copied method's annotations
        for (Annotation annotation : annotations) {
            copiedMethod.addAnnotation(annotation.annotationType());
        }

        // Now you can use the copied method with its annotations
        System.out.println(copiedMethod.getAnnotation(CustomAnnotation.class).value());
        // Output: Example annotation
    }
}

// Define a custom annotation for demonstration purposes
@interface CustomAnnotation {
    String value();
}
```

In this example, the `ExampleClass` contains a method `exampleMethod()` that is annotated with `@Override` and `@CustomAnnotation`. We want to copy this method along with the annotations into a new method called `copiedMethod`. 

To achieve this, we first retrieve the original method using reflection's `getMethod()` method. Then, we get the array of annotations associated with the original method using `getAnnotations()`. 

Next, we create a new method called `copiedMethod` with the same name and parameter types as the original method using `getDeclaredMethod()`. We use `addAnnotation()` to set each of the annotations onto the copied method.

Finally, we can use the copied method and its annotations as desired.

