Which one should I use to store the data in a vector, heap or stack?

Apologies for the misunderstanding.

In this case, you can store the game data objects directly in the vector without using pointers. By doing this, the game data objects will be stored in the stack memory of the vector itself.

Since each game data object is relatively small, it is efficient to store them directly in the vector without the need for dynamic memory allocation and deallocation. This approach simplifies memory management and improves performance.

Here's an example of how you can store game data objects in a vector:

```cpp
#include <vector>

struct GameData {
    wxString name;
    wxString system;
    wxString genre;
    CompletionStatus completionStatus;
    unsigned int finishYear;
    unsigned int startYear;
};

int main() {
    std::vector<GameData> gameList;

    // Add new games to the vector
    GameData game1;
    // Set game1 properties...
    gameList.push_back(game1);

    GameData game2;
    // Set game2 properties...
    gameList.push_back(game2);

    // Access and modify existing games in the vector
    gameList[0].finishYear = 2022;

    // Iterate over the games in the vector
    for (const auto& game : gameList) {
        // Access game properties...
    }

    return 0;
}
```

By storing game data objects directly in the vector, you don't need to manually allocate or deallocate memory. The vector will automatically manage the memory for you.

So, in this case, there is no need to allocate objects on the heap using `new` and store pointers in the vector.

If memory allocation and deallocation are not a concern in your scenario, you could consider storing objects on the heap using pointers and dynamically allocating memory for each game data object. This approach allows you to have more control over the lifetime of each object and to easily manage modifications or deletions.

By storing the game data objects as pointers, you can allocate and deallocate memory dynamically using `new` and `delete`. This can be particularly useful if the game data objects have a large memory footprint or if you need to explicitly control the object's lifespan.

Here's an example of storing game data objects as pointers in a vector:

```cpp
#include <vector>

struct GameData {
    wxString name;
    wxString system;
    wxString genre;
    CompletionStatus completionStatus;
    unsigned int finishYear;
    unsigned int startYear;
};

int main() {
    std::vector<GameData*> gameList;

    // Add new games to the vector
    GameData* game1 = new GameData;
    // Set game1 properties...
    gameList.push_back(game1);

    GameData* game2 = new GameData;
    // Set game2 properties...
    gameList.push_back(game2);

    // Access and modify existing games in the vector
    gameList[0]->finishYear = 2022;

    // Clean up memory by deleting the dynamically allocated objects
    delete gameList[0];
    delete gameList[1];
    gameList.clear();

    return 0;
}
```

Using heap-allocated objects allows you to manage memory explicitly, but it also adds complexity and requires manual memory management. You need to ensure that you deallocate the memory properly to avoid memory leaks.

In summary, if memory allocation and deallocation are not a concern and you require more control over the object's lifespan, storing objects as pointers on the heap can be considered. However, as default practice, storing objects directly in a vector on the stack is simpler, efficient, and less error-prone.

