In C++, you can use the `typeid` and `dynamic_cast` operators along with the `std::tuple` template to iterate over the members of a struct using reflection. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <tuple>
#include <typeinfo>

struct MyStruct {
    int myInt;
    double myDouble;
    std::string myString;
    char myChar;
};

template <typename T, class Tuple, std::size_t N>
struct TupleReflection {
    static void iterate(const Tuple& tuple) {
        std::cout << typeid(T).name() << ": " << dynamic_cast<const T&>(std::get<N>(tuple)) << std::endl;
        TupleReflection<T, Tuple, N - 1>::iterate(tuple);
    }
};

template <typename T, class Tuple>
struct TupleReflection<T, Tuple, 0> {
    static void iterate(const Tuple& tuple) {
        std::cout << typeid(T).name() << ": " << dynamic_cast<const T&>(std::get<0>(tuple)) << std::endl;
    }
};

template <class... Args>
void iterateStruct(const std::tuple<Args...>& tuple) {
    TupleReflection<Args, decltype(tuple), sizeof...(Args) - 1>::iterate(tuple);
}

int main() {
    MyStruct myStruct{42, 3.14, "Hello, world!", 'A'};
    iterateStruct(std::tie(myStruct.myInt, myStruct.myDouble, myStruct.myString, myStruct.myChar));
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we define a `MyStruct` with some members. We then define a template class `TupleReflection` that recursively iterates over the provided `std::tuple` and prints out the type and value of each member using the `typeid` operator and `dynamic_cast`.

The `iterateStruct` function takes the struct members as arguments and creates a tuple from them using `std::tie`. It then calls `TupleReflection` to iterate over the members and print their types and values.

Running the code will output:

```
int: 42
double: 3.14
std::basic_string<char, std::char_traits<char>, std::allocator<char> >: Hello, world!
char: A
```

Note that this approach relies on the struct members being passed in the same order as their declaration in the struct.

