Here is an example of an ATTiny2313 program that generates two tones simultaneously: 300 Hz and 1000 Hz on one output pin.

```c
#include <avr/io.h>
#include <avr/interrupt.h>

volatile uint8_t counter_300Hz = 0;
volatile uint8_t counter_1000Hz = 0;

int main(void) {
    DDRB |= (1 << PB1); // Set PB1 as output pin

    // Timer/Counter1 Setup (16-bit Timer)
    TCCR1B |= (1 << WGM12); // CTC mode
    TCCR1B |= (1 << CS10); // No prescaling
    TIMSK |= (1 << OCIE1A); // Enable Timer/Counter1 compare match A interrupt
    OCR1A = 200; // Set compare value for 300 Hz (16MHz / 2 * 256 * 300)

    // Timer/Counter0 Setup (8-bit Timer)
    TIMSK |= (1 << TOIE0); // Enable Timer/Counter0 overflow interrupt
    TCCR0 |= (1 << CS00); // No prescaling

    sei(); // Enable global interrupts

    while (1) {
        // Main program loop
    }
}

// Timer/Counter1 compare match A interrupt
ISR(TIMER1_COMPA_vect) {
    if (counter_300Hz++ >= 4) {
        PORTB ^= (1 << PB1); // Toggle PB1 pin for 300 Hz
        counter_300Hz = 0;
    }

    if (counter_1000Hz++ >= 1) {
        PORTB ^= (1 << PB1); // Toggle PB1 pin for 1000 Hz
        counter_1000Hz = 0;
    }
}

// Timer/Counter0 overflow interrupt
ISR(TIMER0_OVF_vect) {
    // Perform any necessary operations (if required) in this interrupt routine
}
```

In this program, we use Timer/Counter1 in CTC mode for generating the 300 Hz tone and Timer/Counter0 in normal mode for generating the 1000 Hz tone. The compare match interrupt for Timer/Counter1 is used to toggle the output pin (PB1) every time the appropriate number of timer counts has passed.

Please note that the exact values for OCR1A and the counters may need to be adjusted depending on your system clock frequency and desired frequency accuracy.

Also, make sure to connect a speaker or piezo buzzer to PB1 (pin 15 of ATtiny2313) through a suitable current-limiting resistor to produce audible sound.

Remember to set up the appropriate fuse bits for your ATTiny2313 to configure the clock source and divide factor.

