static std::map<int, std::string> myMap;

// Define and initialize the global std::map variable
std::map<int, std::string> myMap = { {1, "one"}, {2, "two"}, {3, "three"} };

int main() {
    // Access and use the global std::map variable
    for (const auto& pair : myMap) {
        std::cout << pair.first << ": " << pair.second << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we define a global `std::map<int, std::string>` variable named `myMap`. It is initialized with the key-value pairs `{1, "one"}, {2, "two"}, {3, "three"}`. Inside the `main` function, we traverse and print the contents of the `myMap` variable.

