To properly add services in the `Program.cs` file for a generic repository (`GeneralRepository<TEntity>`) and pass the connection string and logger to it, you can follow these steps in an ASP.NET Core Web API 6.0 project:

1. Open the `Program.cs` file in your project.

2. Locate the `CreateHostBuilder` method. Within this method, add the following code to configure the services:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using YourNamespace.Repositories;

public static IHostBuilder CreateHostBuilder(string[] args) =>
    Host.CreateDefaultBuilder(args)
        .ConfigureWebHostDefaults(webBuilder =>
        {
            webBuilder.UseStartup<Startup>();
        })
        .ConfigureServices((hostContext, services) =>
        {
            services.AddScoped(typeof(GeneralRepository<>)); // Register the generic repository

            // Register ILogger<T> where T is the type provided to GeneralRepository<TEntity>
            services.AddLogging(builder =>
            {
                builder.AddConsole();
                builder.AddDebug();
            });

            // Pass the connection string and logger to the GeneralRepository<TEntity> constructor
            services.AddTransient<GeneralRepository<TEntity>>(provider =>
            {
                var connectionString = hostContext.Configuration.GetConnectionString("YourConnectionStringName");
                var logger = provider.GetRequiredService<ILogger<GeneralRepository<TEntity>>>();
                return new GeneralRepository<TEntity>(connectionString, logger);
            });
        });
```

3. Replace `"YourNamespace.Repositories"` with the actual namespace of your repository.

4. Replace `"YourConnectionStringName"` with the name of the connection string you have configured in your `appsettings.json` or other configuration files.

5. Save the `Program.cs` file.

By following the above steps, you will properly register the `GeneralRepository<TEntity>` in the dependency injection container and pass the connection string and logger to its constructor.

Note: Make sure your `GeneralRepository<TEntity>` constructor accepts the connection string and `ILogger<GeneralRepository<TEntity>>` as parameters.

