2. Use listeners with CDI-based injection to develop a JMS-based event message hub.

In this exercise, you will use listeners with CDI-based injection to develop a JMS-based event message hub.

Here's an example of how to create a message hub using JMS and CDI:

```java
@MessageDriven
public class EventMessageHub implements MessageListener {

    @Inject
    private EventDispatcher eventDispatcher;

    @Override
    public void onMessage(Message message) {
        // Process incoming message and dispatch events
        eventDispatcher.dispatch(message.getContent());
    }
}

@ApplicationScoped
public class EventDispatcher {

    @Inject
    private Event<EventData> event;

    public void dispatch(String content) {
        // Create EventData object from content
        EventData eventData = createEventData(content);
        // Fire the event
        event.fire(eventData);
    }

    private EventData createEventData(String content) {
        // Create and populate EventData object
        return new EventData(content);
    }
}

public class EventData {

    private String content;

    public EventData(String content) {
        this.content = content;
    }

    // Getters and setters
}
```

In this example, the `EventMessageHub` class is a JMS message listener. It listens for incoming messages and processes them by dispatching events using the `EventDispatcher` class.

The `EventDispatcher` class uses CDI-based injection to inject the `Event` object, which allows it to fire events. It creates an `EventData` object from the message content and fires the event.

The `EventData` class is a simple POJO that represents the event data.

By using listeners with CDI-based injection, you can develop a flexible and decoupled event-driven architecture.

Make sure to set up the necessary JMS resources and configure the messaging system accordingly to make this example work.

Note: In order to fully understand these examples, you should have knowledge of Java EE, CDI, EJB, JMS, and Jakarta Persistence.

