Sure! Here's a general converter function in Go that can convert two structs with the same fields:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"reflect"
)

type Source struct {
	Field1 string
	Field2 int
	Field3 bool
}

type Destination struct {
	Field1 string
	Field2 int
	Field3 bool
}

func Convert(source Source) Destination {
	dest := Destination{}
	sourceValue := reflect.ValueOf(source)
	destValue := reflect.ValueOf(&dest).Elem()

	for i := 0; i < sourceValue.NumField(); i++ {
		sourceField := sourceValue.Field(i)
		destField := destValue.FieldByName(sourceValue.Type().Field(i).Name)

		if destField.IsValid() && destField.Type() == sourceField.Type() {
			destField.Set(sourceField)
		}
	}

	return dest
}

func main() {
	source := Source{"Value1", 42, true}
	destination := Convert(source)
	fmt.Println("Source:", source)
	fmt.Println("Destination:", destination)
}
```

In this example, we have two structs `Source` and `Destination` with the same fields. The `Convert` function takes a `Source` object and converts it to a `Destination` object by iterating over the fields of the source struct and setting the corresponding fields in the destination struct using reflection.

In the `Convert` function, we use the `reflect` package to get the values and types of the source and destination fields. We then iterate over the source fields and set the corresponding destination fields if they have the same type.

In the `main` function, we create a source object, call the `Convert` function with it, and print both the source and destination objects to see the result.

Note that this general converter assumes that the field names and types are identical between the source and destination structs. If you have structs with different field names or types, you may need to modify the logic accordingly.

