In Kotlin, enum classes require a default constructor for each enum constant. However, if you want to create an enum class with no default constructor for the enum constants, you can define properties and functions in the enum class itself rather than as part of the constructor.

Here's an example:

```kotlin
enum class Weekday {
    MONDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Monday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return false
        }
    },
    TUESDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Tuesday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return false
        }
    },
    WEDNESDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Wednesday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return false
        }
    },
    THURSDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Thursday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return false
        }
    },
    FRIDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Friday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return false
        }
    },
    SATURDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Saturday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return true
        }
    },
    SUNDAY {
        override fun getDayName(): String {
            return "Sunday"
        }

        override fun isWeekend(): Boolean {
            return true
        }
    };

    abstract fun getDayName(): String
    abstract fun isWeekend(): Boolean
}
```

In this example, the `Weekday` enum class has no default constructor. Instead, each enum constant overrides the `getDayName()` and `isWeekend()` functions to provide custom implementations.

You can access these functions as follows:

