    display.setCursor(0, 20);
    display.print(data);
    display.display();

    Wire.beginTransmission(OLED_ADDR);  // Begin I2C transmission to the SSD1306 display
    Wire.write(data);  // Send the same data to the display
    Wire.endTransmission();
  }

  delay(100);  // Adjust the delay as per your requirements
}
```

In this code:

- Ensure that you have the Wire library and the Adafruit_SSD1306 library installed in your Arduino IDE.
- Set `INPUT_DEVICE_ADDRESS` according to the I2C address of the device from which you want to read the data.
- The `setup()` function initializes I2C communication and the SSD1306 display. It also sets up the initial display contents.
- The `loop()` function repeatedly reads one byte of data from the input device using ` Wire.requestFrom()` and reads the data using `Wire.read()`.
- The received data is then updated on the SSD1306 display and written to it using `Wire.beginTransmission()`, `Wire.write()`, and `Wire.endTransmission()`.
- The display is cleared before updating the value to avoid overlapping text on the display.
- The loop continues to repeat with a delay of 100 milliseconds (you can adjust this delay as per your requirements).

Remember to connect the SDA and SCL pins of the input I2C device and the SSD1306 display to the corresponding pins on your Arduino board.

With this code, you should be able to read I2C data from one device and write it to the SSD1306 display using an Arduino. The received data will be displayed on the screen.

