In C++11, you can implement the Singleton pattern using the following steps:

1. Create a class and make its constructor, copy constructor, and assignment operator private to prevent direct instantiation and copying.

2. Provide a static member function that returns a reference to the single instance of the class.

3. Use `std::atomic` and `std::mutex` to ensure thread safety during the creation of the instance.

4. Make sure to use the double-check locking mechanism to avoid unnecessary locking.

Here's an example implementation of the Singleton pattern in C++11:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>

class Singleton {
private:
    static std::atomic<Singleton*> instance;
    static std::mutex mutex;

    Singleton() {
        std::cout << "Singleton instance created" << std::endl;
    }

public:
    Singleton(const Singleton&) = delete;
    Singleton& operator=(const Singleton&) = delete;

    static Singleton* getInstance() {
        Singleton* tmpInstance = instance.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);  // Acquire the instance if already created
        std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_acquire);  // Ensure ordering before instance check

        if (tmpInstance == nullptr) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);  // Lock if instance is still null
            tmpInstance = instance.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);  // Check again after acquiring lock
            if (tmpInstance == nullptr) {
                tmpInstance = new Singleton();  // Create new instance if still null
                std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release);  // Ensure ordering before storing the instance
                instance.store(tmpInstance, std::memory_order_relaxed);  // Store the new instance
            }
        }

        return tmpInstance;
    }

    void showMessage() {
        std::cout << "Hello from Singleton!" << std::endl;
    }
};

std::atomic<Singleton*> Singleton::instance(nullptr);
std::mutex Singleton::mutex;

int main() {
    Singleton* singleton1 = Singleton::getInstance();
    singleton1->showMessage();

    Singleton* singleton2 = Singleton::getInstance();
    singleton2->showMessage();

    // Both singleton1 and singleton2 are pointers to the same instance
    if (singleton1 == singleton2) {
        std::cout << "Both pointers refer to the same instance" << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `getInstance()` function is responsible for creating and returning the single instance of the `Singleton`. The `std::atomic` type is used to guarantee atomic operations on the `instance` pointer, ensuring its safety in a multi-threaded environment. The `std::mutex` type is used to lock the critical section while creating the instance.

To make Hibernate use SQLite as the database, you need to follow these steps:

1. Include the SQLite JDBC driver in your project: Download the SQLite JDBC driver JAR file from the official SQLite website or from a Maven repository and include it in your project's classpath.

2. Configure Hibernate properties: In your Hibernate configuration file (usually named `hibernate.cfg.xml`), configure the following properties:

```xml
<property name="hibernate.dialect">org.hibernate.dialect.SQLiteDialect</property>
<property name="hibernate.connection.driver_class">org.sqlite.JDBC</property>
<property name="hibernate.connection.url">jdbc:sqlite:path/to/database.db</property>
<property name="hibernate.connection.username"></property>
<property name="hibernate.connection.password"></property>
<property name="hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto">update</property>
```

Make sure to adjust the `url` property to point to your SQLite database file's path and name.

3. Create Hibernate session factory: In your Java code, create a `SessionFactory` object using the Hibernate configuration file and the SQLite JDBC driver:

```java
Configuration configuration = new Configuration();
configuration.configure("hibernate.cfg.xml");

ServiceRegistry serviceRegistry = new StandardServiceRegistryBuilder()
    .applySettings(configuration.getProperties())
    .build();

SessionFactory sessionFactory = configuration.buildSessionFactory(serviceRegistry);
```

4. Use the session factory in your application: Once you have the `sessionFactory` object, you can use it to open sessions and perform database operations:

```java
Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();

// Perform database operations using Hibernate APIs
// For example:
MyEntity entity = new MyEntity();
entity.setProperty("value");
session.save(entity);

transaction.commit();
session.close();
```

By following these steps, you can make Hibernate use SQLite as the underlying database for your Java application.

Certainly! Here's an Arduino script that simulates a fire effect using WS2811 with 22 LED strips, excluding the color white.

Before uploading the code to your Arduino board, make sure you have the FastLED library installed. You can install it by navigating to "Sketch" > "Include Library" > "Manage Libraries" and searching for "FastLED."

```cpp
#include <FastLED.h>

// Define the number of LEDs and the data pin
#define NUM_LEDS 22
#define DATA_PIN 5

// Define the LED strip type
#define LED_TYPE WS2811

// Define global variables
CRGB leds[NUM_LEDS];
int flickerSpeed = 20;  // Adjust this for flicker effect speed

// Fire color palette
CRGBPalette16 firePalette = CRGBPalette16(
  CRGB::Black,
  CRGB::Red,
  CRGB::Red,
  CRGB::Red,
  CRGB::Orange,
  CRGB::Orange,
  CRGB::Yellow,
  CRGB::Yellow,
  CRGB::White,  // Exclude this color
  CRGB::Yellow,
  CRGB::Yellow,
  CRGB::Orange,
  CRGB::Orange,
  CRGB::Red,
  CRGB::Red,
  CRGB::Red
);

void setup() {
  // Initialize the LED strip
  FastLED.addLeds<LED_TYPE, DATA_PIN>(leds, NUM_LEDS);

  // Set maximum brightness (optional)
  FastLED.setBrightness(200);
}

void loop() {
  // Call the fire effect function
  fireEffect();

  // Optional: Add delays or other effects here
}

void fireEffect() {
  // Update the LED strip with the fire effect
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_LEDS; i++) {
    // Generate random brightness for flickering effect
    int flicker = random(170, 255);

    // Set the LED color and brightness using the fire palette
    leds[i] = ColorFromPalette(firePalette, random8(), flicker, LINEARBLEND);
  }

  // Show the updated LED strip
  FastLED.show();

  // Delay for the fire effect
  delay(flickerSpeed);
}
```

This code uses the FastLED library to control the WS2811 LED strips and defines a fire color palette consisting of various shades of red, orange, yellow, and excluding white. The `fireEffect` function generates random brightness levels for each LED and sets its color using the fire palette. The LED strip is then updated and shown, followed by a delay to control the flickering effect.

