Here's an example code implementation for the `list.insert()` function in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <list>

int main() {
    std::list<int> myList;

    // Insert elements at the beginning of the list
    myList.insert(myList.begin(), 5);    // [5]
    myList.insert(myList.begin(), 10);   // [10, 5]

    // Insert elements at the end of the list
    myList.insert(myList.end(), 15);     // [10, 5, 15]
    myList.insert(myList.end(), 20);     // [10, 5, 15, 20]

    // Insert elements at a specific position in the list
    std::list<int>::iterator it = myList.begin();
    std::advance(it, 2);                 // Move iterator to the third position
    myList.insert(it, 25);               // [10, 5, 25, 15, 20]

    // Insert elements by repeating a value multiple times
    myList.insert(myList.end(), 3, 30);  // [10, 5, 25, 15, 20, 30, 30, 30]

    // Print the list
    for (int num : myList) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code demonstrates the usage of `list.insert()` function in C++. It inserts elements at the beginning, end, and a specific position in the list. It also shows how to insert elements by repeating a value multiple times. Finally, it prints the list elements to the console.

The `list` class in C++ provides an `insert()` member function that allows you to insert elements at various positions in the list. Here's the method signature and usage examples:

```cpp
iterator insert (const_iterator position, const T& value);
iterator insert (const_iterator position, T&& value);
iterator insert (const_iterator position, size_type count, const T& value);
template <class InputIterator>
iterator insert (const_iterator position, InputIterator first, InputIterator last);
```

- The first version inserts a single copy of the value `value` before the iterator `position` and returns an iterator pointing to the inserted element.
- The second version moves the value `value` before the iterator `position` and returns an iterator pointing to the inserted element.
- The third version inserts `count` copies of the value `value` before the iterator `position` and returns an iterator pointing to the first inserted element.
- The fourth version inserts elements from the range `[first, last)` before the iterator `position` and returns an iterator pointing to the first inserted element.

Here's an example code demonstrating the usage of these `list.insert()` methods:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <list>

int main() {
    std::list<int> myList;

    // Insert a single element
    myList.insert(myList.begin(), 5);  // [5]

    // Insert elements using a range
    std::list<int> secondList = {10, 15, 20};
    myList.insert(myList.end(), secondList.begin(), secondList.end());  // [5, 10, 15, 20]

    // Insert multiple copies of a value
    myList.insert(myList.begin(), 3, 2);  // [2, 2, 2, 5, 10, 15, 20]

    // Print the list
    for (int num : myList) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we create a list `myList` and demonstrate the usage of the different `insert()` methods. We insert a single element, insert elements using a range from another list, and insert multiple copies of a value. Finally, we print the list elements to the console.

