SQL within PL/SQL:
In PL/SQL, you can execute SQL statements within your PL/SQL code. This allows you to interact with the database and manipulate data directly. SQL statements can be executed using embedded SQL or dynamic SQL.

Embedded SQL is when SQL statements are written directly within the PL/SQL code. Here is an example:

```sql
DECLARE
   variable_name datatype;
BEGIN
   SELECT column_name INTO variable_name FROM table_name WHERE condition;
   -- code to process the retrieved data
END;
```

In this example, the SELECT statement is embedded within the PL/SQL block, and the result of the query is stored in the variable variable_name.

Dynamic SQL is when SQL statements are constructed dynamically at runtime using strings and executed using the EXECUTE IMMEDIATE statement. Here is an example:

```sql
DECLARE
   variable_name datatype;
   sql_statement VARCHAR2(100);
BEGIN
   sql_statement := 'SELECT column_name FROM table_name WHERE condition';
   EXECUTE IMMEDIATE sql_statement INTO variable_name;
   -- code to process the retrieved data
END;
```

In this example, the SQL statement is constructed dynamically and stored in the variable sql_statement. Then, the EXECUTE IMMEDIATE statement is used to execute the SQL statement and store the result in the variable variable_name.

Composite Data Types in PL/SQL:
Composite data types in PL/SQL allow you to create your own record or object types to store related data. There are two types of composite data types in PL/SQL: record types and table types.

Record types are used to define a structure that contains multiple fields of different data types. Here is an example:

```sql
DECLARE
   TYPE employee_type IS RECORD (
      id NUMBER,
      name VARCHAR2(100),
      salary NUMBER
   );
   employee employee_type;
BEGIN
   employee.id := 1;
   employee.name := 'John Doe';
   employee.salary := 5000;
   -- code to process the employee record
END;
```

In this example, a record type named employee_type is defined, which contains fields for id, name, and salary. An employee record is then declared using the employee_type and populated with data.

Table types are used to define a collection of records. They can be used for bulk processing and storing multiple rows of data. Here is an example:

```sql
DECLARE
   TYPE employee_list IS TABLE OF employee_type;
   employees employee_list;
BEGIN
   -- code to populate the employees table type
   FOR i IN employees.FIRST..employees.LAST LOOP
      DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(employees(i).id || ' ' || employees(i).name || ' ' || employees(i).salary);
   END LOOP;
END;
```

In this example, a table type named employee_list is defined, which is a collection of employee_type records. An employees table type is declared and can be populated with multiple employee records. The data in the table type can be accessed using a loop or other constructs.

Cursor Management in PL/SQL:
In PL/SQL, cursors are used to retrieve and process SQL query results. There are two types of cursors in PL/SQL: implicit cursors and explicit cursors.

Implicit cursors are automatically created by the PL/SQL compiler to handle SQL statements that are not assigned to any explicit cursor. They are used for single-row queries or when you only expect a single row to be returned. Here is an example:

```sql
DECLARE
   variable_name datatype;
BEGIN
   SELECT column_name INTO variable_name FROM table_name WHERE condition;
   -- code to process the retrieved data
END;
```

In this example, an implicit cursor is used to retrieve a single row of data from the table using the SELECT statement. The result is stored in the variable variable_name for further processing.

Explicit cursors, on the other hand, are declared and defined explicitly by the programmer. They are used for more complex queries that return multiple rows of data. Here is an example:

```sql
DECLARE
   CURSOR cursor_name IS SELECT column_name FROM table_name WHERE condition;
   variable_name datatype;
BEGIN
   OPEN cursor_name;
   LOOP
      FETCH cursor_name INTO variable_name;
      EXIT WHEN cursor_name%NOTFOUND;
      -- code to process the retrieved data
   END LOOP;
   CLOSE cursor_name;
END;
```

In this example, an explicit cursor named cursor_name is declared and defined to select multiple rows of data from the table. The cursor is then opened, and a loop is used to fetch each row into the variable variable_name. The loop continues until there are no more rows to fetch, and the cursor is finally closed.

Overall, cursor management in PL/SQL allows you to retrieve and process query results using implicit and explicit cursors, depending on the complexity and requirements of your queries.

