To create a period meter using Arduino, you can use a basic sketch like the one below. This sketch measures the time between consecutive positive pulses on a digital input pin and calculates the period.

```cpp
const int inputPin = 2;       // Digital pin connected to input signal
unsigned long pulseStartTime; // Stores the time of the first pulse
unsigned long period;         // Stores the calculated period

void setup() {
  pinMode(inputPin, INPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  if (digitalRead(inputPin) == HIGH) {
    pulseStartTime = micros(); // Record the time of the first pulse
  }
  else if (pulseStartTime != 0) {
    period = micros() - pulseStartTime; // Calculate the period
    pulseStartTime = 0; // Reset the pulse start time
    Serial.print("Period: ");
    Serial.print(period, DEC);
    Serial.println(" microseconds");
  }
}
```

This sketch assumes that the input signal is connected to digital pin 2. Whenever a positive pulse is observed on this pin, the time is recorded as the pulse start time. On the subsequent falling edge, the current time is subtracted from the pulse start time to calculate the period. The period value is then displayed on the serial monitor at a baud rate of 9600.

Make sure to upload this sketch to your Arduino board and connect the input signal accordingly. After running the sketch, you should see the period being displayed in microseconds whenever a pulse is detected.

