In order to create a stored procedure with .NET, you will need to use a database access library such as ADO.NET or Entity Framework. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a stored procedure using ADO.NET:

1. Open Visual Studio and create a new project.
2. Add a reference to the ADO.NET library by right-clicking on the project in the Solution Explorer, selecting "Add Reference," and then selecting the appropriate ADO.NET library.
3. Create a new class to encapsulate your stored procedure logic.
4. Declare a connection string to connect to your database. This can be done through the web.config or app.config file.
5. Create a method to execute the stored procedure. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System.Data.SqlClient;

public class MyStoredProcedures
{
    private string connectionString = "YourConnectionStringHere";

    public void ExecuteStoredProcedure()
    {
        using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
        {
            connection.Open();

            using (SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand("YourStoredProcedureName", connection))
            {
                command.CommandType = System.Data.CommandType.StoredProcedure;

                // Set any input parameters here
                command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@ParameterName", value);

                // Execute the stored procedure
                command.ExecuteNonQuery();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

6. You can call this method from your application code to execute the stored procedure.

Note: Remember to replace `"YourConnectionStringHere"` with your actual connection string and `"YourStoredProcedureName"` with the name of your stored procedure. Also, make sure to define any input and output parameters as needed and handle any exceptions that may occur during the execution of the stored procedure.

To create a stored procedure with Entity Framework (EF) in .NET, you can follow these steps:

1. Open Visual Studio and create a new project.
2. Install the Entity Framework package via NuGet or ensure it is already installed in your project.
3. Create an Entity Data Model (.edmx) file by right-clicking on your project in the Solution Explorer, selecting "Add," and then selecting "New Item." Choose "Data" from the left menu and select "ADO.NET Entity Data Model." Follow the wizard to generate the .edmx file based on your database schema.
4. Open the .edmx file and right-click on the designer surface. Select "Update Model from Database" to refresh the model and include any new or modified stored procedures.
5. In the Function Import window, select the stored procedure you want to import. Provide a method name and specify the return type (if any) for the procedure. Click "Finish" to import the stored procedure to your model.
6. Open the auto-generated code file (usually ends with `.Context.tt`) associated with your .edmx file. This file will contain the DBContext and entities related to the database schema.
7. Inside the DBContext class, add a new method to execute your stored procedure. Here's an example:

```csharp
public void ExecuteStoredProcedure()
{
    using (var context = new YourDBContext())
    {
        context.Database.ExecuteSqlCommand("YourStoredProcedureName");
    }
}
```

8. You can call this method from your application code to execute the stored procedure.

Note: Remember to replace `"YourDBContext"` with the name of your EF DBContext class and `"YourStoredProcedureName"` with the name of your stored procedure. Also, make sure to handle any exceptions that may occur during the execution of the stored procedure.

In Vue 3, the Options API has been replaced with the Composition API, which allows for a more flexible and modular approach to developing components. However, the concept of watching props remains the same.

To watch props in Vue 3 using the Composition API, you can use the `watch` function provided by the Vue 3 `@vue/composition-api` package. Here's an example of how you can use it:

First, make sure you have the `@vue/composition-api` package installed in your project:

```bash
npm install @vue/composition-api
```

Next, import the necessary functions from the package and use them in your component:

```javascript
// Import necessary functions from @vue/composition-api package
import { ref, onMounted, watch } from '@vue/composition-api';

export default {
  props: ['myProp'], // Define the prop you want to watch

  setup(props) {
    const myPropValue = ref(props.myProp); // Create a reactive reference to store the prop value

    // Watch for changes in the prop value
    watch(() => props.myProp, (newValue) => {
      myPropValue.value = newValue;
      console.log('Prop value changed:', newValue);
    });

    // Other setup logic

    // Return variables or functions that can be used in the component template
    return {
      myPropValue,
    };
  },
};
```

In the above example, we define a prop called `myProp` in the `props` array. We then use the `ref` function to create a reactive reference called `myPropValue` and initialize it with the initial value of the prop. The `watch` function is used to monitor changes in the prop value. Whenever the prop value changes, the provided callback function is executed. In this example, we update the `myPropValue` reference and log the new value to the console.

Finally, don't forget to include the `setup` function in your component. This is where you can define reactive variables, create computed properties, and register lifecycle hooks.

Note that the `setup` function is a special function in Vue 3's Composition API. It replaces the options object used in Vue 2's Options API and allows you to define reactive data and watch for changes.

