Preventing a C++ program from being killed (e.g., using the "TASKKILL /F" command or through Task Manager) in Windows is not recommended as it goes against the user's control over their own system. Terminating a program through these means is a standard procedure in case a program becomes unresponsive or behaves incorrectly.

However, if you want to handle a graceful termination of your C++ program, you can implement signal handling in your code. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <csignal>

using namespace std;

// Signal handler function
void signalHandler(int signal) {
    switch (signal) {
        case SIGINT:
            cout << "Program terminated by user." << endl;
            // Perform any necessary cleanup
            exit(0);
        default:
            break;
    }
}

int main() {
    // Register the signal handler
    signal(SIGINT, signalHandler);

    // Your program logic here

    // Ensure the program runs indefinitely
    while (true) {
        // ...
    }

    // Return a success status
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `signalHandler` function is defined to handle the `SIGINT` signal sent by the operating system when the user interrupts the program (e.g., pressing Ctrl+C). You can then perform necessary cleanup tasks and terminate the program gracefully.

Keep in mind that attempting to prevent a program from being terminated can have unintended consequences and may not be feasible in some cases. It's generally best to rely on the user's ability to terminate a program when needed.

