In .NET, the starting point of a program is typically the Main method. The Main method is the entry point of a .NET application and is where the program execution begins.

The Main method is defined in a class and has the following signature:

```
static void Main(string[] args)
```

The Main method can be found in the Program.cs file, which is typically created by default in many .NET project templates. However, the Main method can be in any class as long as it is declared as static.

In the Main method, you can write the initial code that runs when the program starts. This code can include initializing variables, calling methods, and interacting with the user or external resources.

Here's a simple example of a Main method:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");

        // Additional code goes here

        Console.ReadLine(); // Wait for user input before closing the console
    }
}
```

In this example, the Main method prints "Hello, World!" to the console and then waits for the user to press the Enter key before closing the console.

Note: In some specific .NET applications like ASP.NET, Windows Forms, or WPF applications, there might be an Application class or a specialized entry point instead of the Main method. However, the concept of having a starting point for program execution remains similar.

