#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool isKGoodNumber(int num, int k) {
    bool digits[k+1];
    for (int i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
        digits[i] = false;
    }

    while (num > 0) {
        digits[num % 10] = true;
        num /= 10;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
        if (!digits[i]) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}

int main() {
    int n, k;
    scanf("%d %d", &n, &k);

    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int num;
        scanf("%d", &num);
        if (isKGoodNumber(num, k)) {
            count++;
        }
    }

    printf("%d\n", count);

    return 0;
}
```

The `isKGoodNumber` function checks if a given number is a k-good number. It initializes an array `digits` of size `k+1` to false. Then, it iteratively sets the corresponding index in the `digits` array to true for each digit in the given number. Finally, it checks if all digits from 0 to k have been encountered in the number.

In the `main` function, we read the values of `n` and `k` from input and iterate through the array of numbers. For each number, we use the `isKGoodNumber` function to determine if it is a k-good number. If it is, we increment the count. Finally, we print the count of k-good numbers.

In Express, you can handle exceptions using middleware functions. Here's an example of how you can create an exception handler middleware:

```javascript
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err); // Log the error
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong' }); // Return a generic error response
});
```

Make sure to define this middleware after you have defined all your other routes and middleware. The error handler middleware has four parameters: `err`, `req`, `res`, and `next`. The `err` parameter holds the error object, `req` is the request object, `res` is the response object, and `next` is a function to pass control to the next middleware.

To use the error handler, you can throw an error in any middleware or route handler by calling `next(err)`. For example:

```javascript
app.get('/', (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    // Some code that may throw an error
    throw new Error('Something went wrong');
  } catch (err) {
    next(err); // Forward the error to the error handler
  }
});
```

The error handler middleware will catch the error, log it, and send a generic error response with a status code of 500.

You can customize the error handler middleware to suit your needs, such as sending different error responses based on the type of error or returning stack traces in development mode.

If you want to throw an error based on the response, you can create a custom error class and throw it in your route handlers. Here's an example:

```javascript
class CustomError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
  }
}

app.get('/', (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    // Some code that may generate a response error
    const response = { success: false, errorMessage: 'Something went wrong' };
    if (!response.success) {
      throw new CustomError(response.errorMessage, 400); // Throw a custom error with a specific status code
    }
    res.json(response);
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);
  res.status(err.statusCode || 500).json({ error: err.message || 'Something went wrong' });
});
```

In this example, we created a custom error class `CustomError` that extends the `Error` class. It takes two parameters: the error message and the status code. In the route handler, if the response indicates an error condition, we throw the custom error with an appropriate message and status code. The error handler middleware then catches the custom error and sends the corresponding response.

You can customize the `CustomError` class to include additional properties or methods as needed. You can also modify the error handler middleware to customize the response based on the custom error properties.

To get the status code of an error generated by Express, you can check the `statusCode` property of the error object. Here's an example:

```javascript
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500; // Get the status code from the error object, default to 500 if undefined

  res.status(statusCode).json({ error: err.message || 'Something went wrong' });
});
```

In this example, the error handler middleware checks the `statusCode` property of the error object. If it is defined, it uses that as the status code of the response. Otherwise, it defaults to 500 (Internal Server Error).

