There is an error in the code. The issue is with the `onRCInputChange` function. The `pulseIn` function is blocking, meaning it waits for the pulse duration to complete before returning. This causes the Arduino to freeze and prevents the LED modes from changing.

To fix this, you can use a non-blocking approach using `pulseInLong` function. Here's the updated code:

```cpp
void onRCInputChange() {
  static unsigned long lastPulseTime = 0;
  static int lastMode = -1;

  // Read the pulse duration of the receiver input signal
  unsigned long pulseDuration = pulseInLong(RC_INPUT_PIN, HIGH, 20000);

  if (pulseDuration != 0) {
    if (pulseDuration > 1100 && pulseDuration < 1400) {
      mode = 0; // Position 1 on the switch
    } else if (pulseDuration > 1500 && pulseDuration < 1800) {
      mode = 1; // Position 2 on the switch
    } else if (pulseDuration > 1900 && pulseDuration < 2100) {
      mode = 2; // Position 3 on the switch
    }

    // Only update the mode if it has changed
    if (lastMode != mode) {
      lastMode = mode;

      // Reset the fire simulation
      memset(heat, 0, sizeof(heat));
    }

    lastPulseTime = millis();
  }

  // If no pulse has been received for more than 1 second, assume the switch is off
  if (millis() - lastPulseTime > 1000) {
    mode = 0; // Position 1 on the switch
    lastMode = mode;

    // Reset the fire simulation
    memset(heat, 0, sizeof(heat));
  }
}
```

This updated code uses a static variable `lastPulseTime` to keep track of the time of the last received pulse and `lastMode` to keep track of the last mode. If no pulse has been received for more than 1 second, it assumes the switch is off and resets the mode to position 1.

Additionally, the `heat` array is now declared as a static variable inside the `Fire2012WithPalette` function so that it retains its values between function calls.

