Yes, there are wasm runtimes that support serialization of the entire execution state to be resumed later. One such runtime is Wasmtime, which is a standalone WebAssembly runtime developed by the Bytecode Alliance. Wasmtime provides an API that allows you to serialize the state of a running WebAssembly instance to a binary blob, which can be stored or transferred, and later deserialized to resume the execution from the exact point it was serialized.

Here's an example using the Rust programming language and the Wasmtime runtime to serialize and deserialize the execution state:

```rust
use wasmtime::*;

fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    let store = Store::default();
    let module = Module::from_file(&store, "path/to/your/wasm/module.wasm")?;
    let instance = Instance::new(&store, &module, &[])?;

    // Run some operations on the WebAssembly instance...

    // Serialize the execution state
    let serialized_state = instance.context_mut().serialize()?;

    // Store or transfer the serialized_state...

    // Later, deserialize and resume the execution state
    let instance = Instance::new_from_serialized(&store, &module, serialized_state)?;

    // Continue execution from the exact point it was serialized...

    Ok(())
}
```

This is just a basic example to demonstrate the serialization and deserialization capabilities. You can find more complex usage examples and detailed documentation in the Wasmtime documentation.

