To reduce the size of images, you typically need to manipulate or compress them on the server-side before serving them to the client. This requires backend programming or image processing techniques.

One approach could be to send a request to the server-side with specific parameters indicating the desired image size or format. The server-side code would then handle the resizing or compression based on these parameters.

For example, if the server-side is using a programming language like PHP, you could send a request with query parameters specifying the desired image dimensions or compression level. The server-side code could then resize or compress the image accordingly before sending it back as the response.

Here's a simplified example using PHP:

```php
<?php
// Server-side code (PHP)
$imagePath = "../path/to/image.jpg";
$width = $_GET['width'];
$height = $_GET['height'];
$quality = $_GET['quality'];

// Resize and compress the image using a library like GD or ImageMagick
// ...
// Code here to manipulate the image based on the provided parameters

// Set appropriate Content-Type header
header('Content-Type: image/jpeg');

// Output the manipulated image
// ...
// Code here to output the modified image file
?>
```

On the client-side, you would make a request to the server-side with the desired parameters in the URL. For example:

```html
<!-- Client-side code (HTML) -->
<img src="https://example.com/image.php?width=500&height=300&quality=80" alt="Image">
```

This example shows how you can pass parameters in the URL to modify or compress images on the server-side before serving them to the client. You would need to implement the server-side code in your preferred programming language and use image processing libraries to achieve the desired results.

