To plot a curve using the C programming language, we need to use a graphics library that supports graphical rendering. One of the popular libraries for this purpose is the Simple DirectMedia Layer (SDL). Here's an example of how to plot a curve using SDL in C:

```c
#include <SDL2/SDL.h>

// Function to plot the curve
void plotCurve(SDL_Renderer* renderer)
{
    SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(renderer, 255, 255, 255, SDL_ALPHA_OPAQUE); // Set color to white
    SDL_RenderClear(renderer); // Clear the renderer

    // Loop through the x-coordinates and calculate corresponding y-coordinates
    for (int x = 0; x < 800; x++)
    {
        int y = 200 * sin(x / 50.0) + 200; // Example curve function: y = 200 * sin(x/50) + 200

        SDL_RenderDrawPoint(renderer, x, y); // Plot the point on the renderer
    }

    SDL_RenderPresent(renderer); // Present the rendered curve
}

int main()
{
    SDL_Window* window = NULL;
    SDL_Renderer* renderer = NULL;

    SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO); // Initialize SDL

    // Create a window and renderer
    SDL_CreateWindowAndRenderer(800, 600, SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN, &window, &renderer);

    // Plot the curve
    plotCurve(renderer);

    // Wait for the user to close the window
    SDL_Event event;
    while (SDL_WaitEvent(&event))
    {
        if (event.type == SDL_QUIT)
            break;
    }

    // Cleanup and exit
    SDL_DestroyRenderer(renderer);
    SDL_DestroyWindow(window);
    SDL_Quit();

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, SDL is used to initialize a window and a renderer. The `plotCurve()` function loops through the x-coordinates and calculates the corresponding y-coordinates using a given function (in this case, `y = 200 * sin(x/50) + 200`). The points are then plotted on the renderer using `SDL_RenderDrawPoint()`. Finally, the rendered curve is presented on the window using `SDL_RenderPresent()`.

Note that this is just a simple example to demonstrate plotting a curve using SDL in C. You can modify the `plotCurve()` function with your own curve calculation or use different libraries or approaches depending on your requirements.

