To use `options.AddPolicy` with multiple policies in .NET, you can follow these steps:

1. In your `Startup.cs` file, locate the `ConfigureServices` method.

2. Inside the `ConfigureServices` method, add the following code to configure your policies:

```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    // Add your other service configurations...

    services.AddAuthorization(options =>
    {
        options.AddPolicy("Policy1", policy =>
        {
            policy.RequireAuthenticatedUser();
            // Add any other requirements or conditions for Policy1
        });

        options.AddPolicy("Policy2", policy =>
        {
            policy.RequireRole("Admin");
            // Add any other requirements or conditions for Policy2
        });
    });

    // Add your other service configurations...
}
```

3. With the code above, two policies "Policy1" and "Policy2" are added using `options.AddPolicy`. Each policy has its own requirements and conditions specified inside the lambda expression.

4. To use the policies in your controllers or action methods, you can use the `[Authorize]` attribute with the policy name:

```csharp
[Authorize("Policy1")]
public IActionResult SomeAction()
{
    // Your code here
}

[Authorize("Policy2")]
public IActionResult AnotherAction()
{
    // Your code here
}
```

In the above example, the `[Authorize]` attribute is used to apply the corresponding policy to each action method.

By using `options.AddPolicy` with multiple policies, you can have fine-grained control over authorization requirements in your .NET application.

