To achieve your desired functionality, you will need to connect the external 10k potentiometer to the AT-Mega32 microcontroller and use it to control the intensity of the white LEDs.

Here's a step-by-step guide to achieve this:

1. Connect the center terminal of the external 10k potentiometer (pot) to an Analog input pin of the AT-Mega32 (e.g., ADC0).
2. Connect one of the outer terminals of the pot to the GND (ground) pin of the AT-Mega32.
3. Connect the other outer terminal of the pot to the 5V supply on your board.

Now you can use the microcontroller to read the value of the potentiometer and control the LEDs accordingly. Here's a sample code to get you started:

```c
#include <avr/io.h>

void init_ADC()
{
    // Set reference voltage to AVCC and left adjust the result
    ADMUX = (1<<REFS0) | (1<<ADLAR);

    // Enable ADC, enable ADC interrupt, and set ADC clock prescaler to 128
    ADCSRA = (1<<ADEN) | (1<<ADIE) | (1<<ADPS2) | (1<<ADPS1) | (1<<ADPS0);

    // Start the first conversion
    ADCSRA |= (1<<ADSC);
}

int main(void)
{
    // Set all LEDs as outputs
    DDRD = 0xFF;

    // Initialize the ADC
    init_ADC();

    // Infinite loop
    while (1) 
    {
        // Wait for ADC conversion to complete
        while(ADCSRA & (1<<ADSC));

        // Read the ADC value
        uint8_t adc_value = ADCH;

        // Perform necessary calculations to control the LEDs based on potentiometer value
        uint8_t num_leds = adc_value / 25;  // Divide ADC value by 25 to get 0-10 range

        // Turn on/off LEDs based on potentiometer value
        PORTD = (0xFF << (8 - num_leds));

        // Start the next conversion
        ADCSRA |= (1<<ADSC);
    }
}

// ADC conversion complete interrupt
ISR(ADC_vect)
{
    // ADC conversion is complete
}
```

This code initializes the ADC and continuously reads the ADC value from the external potentiometer. The ADC value is then divided by 25 to get a range from 0 to 10. Based on this value, the corresponding number of LEDs are turned on. For example, if the potentiometer value is at maximum, all LEDs will be turned on.

Make sure to connect the 6 white LEDs to appropriate output pins on the AT-Mega32 (e.g., PORTD in the example code).

Please note that you might need to adjust the code and connections according to your specific board and microcontroller pin configuration.

