To parse the CSV data without using an external library, we can manually split the CSV string into rows and columns. Here's an updated version of the `processUpload` function that performs manual CSV parsing:

```javascript
const processUpload = (event) => {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let fileData;
    if (event.isBase64Encoded) {
      fileData = Buffer.from(event.body, "base64").toString("utf-8");
    } else {
      fileData = event.body;
    }

    if (!fileData) {
      reject(new Error("No file uploaded"));
    }

    const rows = fileData.split(/\r?\n/); // Split file into rows
    const records = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < rows.length; i++) {
      const row = rows[i];
      if (row.trim() !== "") {
        const columns = row.split(","); // Split each row into columns
        records.push(columns);
      }
    }

    const fileBuffer = Buffer.from(fileData, "utf-8");
    const file = {
      fieldname: "file",
      originalname: "upload.csv",
      encoding: "utf-8",
      mimetype: "text/csv",
      buffer: fileBuffer,
      size: fileBuffer.length,
      records: records, // Add the parsed records as a property
    };

    resolve(file);
  });
};
```

In this updated code snippet, we manually split the CSV data string into rows using the regular expression `/\r?\n/`. Then, for each row, we split it into columns using a comma (`,`) as the delimiter. The resulting columns are pushed into the `records` array.

Now, when you access the `records` property of the resolved `file` object, it should contain an array of arrays, where each inner array represents a row from the CSV file, with its corresponding columns.

