To get the size of a folder in megabytes (MB) using C#, you can use the `DirectoryInfo` class along with the `FileInfo` class. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string folderPath = "C:\\path\\to\\folder";

        try
        {
            // Get the size of the folder
            long folderSizeInBytes = GetFolderSize(folderPath);

            // Convert the size to megabytes
            double folderSizeInMB = folderSizeInBytes / (1024.0 * 1024.0);

            Console.WriteLine($"Size of folder: {folderSizeInMB} MB");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Error: " + ex.Message);
        }
    }

    static long GetFolderSize(string folderPath)
    {
        DirectoryInfo directoryInfo = new DirectoryInfo(folderPath);
        long folderSize = 0;

        // Calculate size of files in the folder
        FileInfo[] files = directoryInfo.GetFiles();
        foreach (FileInfo file in files)
        {
            folderSize += file.Length;
        }

        // Calculate size of sub-folders recursively
        DirectoryInfo[] subDirectories = directoryInfo.GetDirectories();
        foreach (DirectoryInfo subDirectory in subDirectories)
        {
            folderSize += GetFolderSize(subDirectory.FullName);
        }

        return folderSize;
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace `"C:\\path\\to\\folder"` with the actual folder path you want to measure the size of.

This program calculates the size of the specified folder by summing up the sizes of all the files in the folder using `FileInfo.Length`. It also recursively traverses through any sub-folders in the directory using `DirectoryInfo.GetDirectories` and calls the `GetFolderSize` method recursively to calculate the size of each sub-folder. Finally, it converts the folder size from bytes to megabytes by dividing it by `1024.0 * 1024.0`.

