Here is an example code to impose the GDI effect color filter red in C++:

```cpp
#include <Windows.h>

int main()
{
    // Get the device context of the screen
    HDC hdcScreen = GetDC(NULL);

    // Create a color filter matrix to set all other colors to 0 (except red)
    COLORADJUSTMENT ca;
    ZeroMemory(&ca, sizeof(COLORADJUSTMENT));
    ca.caSize = sizeof(COLORADJUSTMENT);
    ca.caFlags = CA_NEGATIVE;
    ca.caRedGamma = 1000;
    ca.caGreenGamma = 0;
    ca.caBlueGamma = 0;

    // Apply the color filter matrix
    SetColorAdjustment(hdcScreen, &ca);

    // Release the device context
    ReleaseDC(NULL, hdcScreen);

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the Windows GDI library to achieve the GDI effect color filter. It first gets the device context of the screen using `GetDC(NULL)`. Then, it creates a `COLORADJUSTMENT` structure that defines a color filter matrix to set all other colors to 0, leaving only the red color. The `SetColorAdjustment` function is then used to apply the color adjustment to the screen. Finally, the device context is released using `ReleaseDC(NULL, hdcScreen)`.

Here is an example code that imposes a GDI translucent effect color filter red on the desktop using `BitBlt` or `StretchBlt` in C++:

```cpp
#include <Windows.h>

int main()
{
    // Get the device context of the screen
    HDC hdcScreen = GetDC(NULL);

    // Get the dimensions of the screen
    int screenWidth = GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN);
    int screenHeight = GetSystemMetrics(SM_CYSCREEN);

    // Create a memory DC and bitmap to hold the desktop image
    HDC hdcMem = CreateCompatibleDC(hdcScreen);
    HBITMAP hBitmap = CreateCompatibleBitmap(hdcScreen, screenWidth, screenHeight);
    SelectObject(hdcMem, hBitmap);

    // Copy the desktop image to the memory DC
    BitBlt(hdcMem, 0, 0, screenWidth, screenHeight, hdcScreen, 0, 0, SRCCOPY);

    // Create a semi-transparent red brush
    HBRUSH hTranslucentRedBrush = CreateSolidBrush(RGB(255, 0, 0));

    // Create a translucent effect by using AlphaBlend with a low alpha value
    BLENDFUNCTION blendFunc;
    blendFunc.BlendOp = AC_SRC_OVER;
    blendFunc.BlendFlags = 0;
    blendFunc.SourceConstantAlpha = 128; // Change this value for different transparency levels (0-255)
    blendFunc.AlphaFormat = AC_SRC_ALPHA;

    // Apply the translucent effect to the desktop image
    if (StretchBlt(hdcMem, 0, 0, screenWidth, screenHeight, hdcMem, 0, 0, screenWidth, screenHeight, SRCCOPY))
    {
        // Apply the red color filter using AlphaBlend
        AlphaBlend(hdcMem, 0, 0, screenWidth, screenHeight, hdcMem, 0, 0, screenWidth, screenHeight, blendFunc);

        // Copy the modified desktop image back to the screen
        BitBlt(hdcScreen, 0, 0, screenWidth, screenHeight, hdcMem, 0, 0, SRCCOPY);
    }

    // Clean up resources
    DeleteObject(hTranslucentRedBrush);
    DeleteObject(hBitmap);
    DeleteDC(hdcMem);

    // Release the device context
    ReleaseDC(NULL, hdcScreen);

    return 0;
}
```

This code first gets the device context of the screen using `GetDC(NULL)` and the dimensions of the screen using `GetSystemMetrics`. Then, it creates a memory DC and a compatible bitmap to hold the desktop image. The desktop image is copied to the memory DC using `BitBlt`. Next, a semi-transparent red brush is created using `CreateSolidBrush`. The translucent effect is applied to the desktop image by using `StretchBlt` to scale the desktop image and then `AlphaBlend` to apply the red color filter with the desired transparency level. Finally, the modified desktop image is copied back to the screen using `BitBlt`. Remember to adjust the `SourceConstantAlpha` value in the `BLENDFUNCTION` structure to change the transparency level of the color filter.

