You can use the SharePoint REST API to check if a file exists on a modern SharePoint page. Here's a sample Java code to achieve this:

```java
import java.net.HttpURLConnection;
import java.net.URL;

public class SharePointPageChecker {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String siteUrl = "https://your-sharepoint-site-url";
        String filePath = "/sites/your-site/Shared%20Documents/file.txt";

        try {
            // Construct the REST API URL
            String apiUrl = siteUrl + "/_api/web/GetFileByServerRelativeUrl('" + filePath + "')";

            URL url = new URL(apiUrl);
            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
            connection.setRequestMethod("GET");
            connection.setRequestProperty("Accept", "application/json");

            // Check if the file exists
            int responseCode = connection.getResponseCode();
            if (responseCode == HttpURLConnection.HTTP_OK) {
                System.out.println("File exists.");
            } else if (responseCode == HttpURLConnection.HTTP_NOT_FOUND) {
                System.out.println("File does not exist.");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Error occurred: " + responseCode);
            }

            connection.disconnect();
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            ex.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace `your-sharepoint-site-url` with the URL of your SharePoint site and `your-site` and `file.txt` with the appropriate values in the `filePath` variable.

This code sends a GET request to the SharePoint REST API endpoint for the file and checks the response code. If the file exists, it will return 200 (HTTP_OK), otherwise it will return 404 (HTTP_NOT_FOUND).

