1. Using a counter variable:
You can use a counter variable to keep track of the number of times the loop has executed and terminate the loop when the counter reaches a certain limit.
```csharp
int limit = 10; // specify the maximum number of iterations
int counter = 0; // initialize counter

while (counter < limit)
{
    // your code here

    counter++; // increment the counter
}
```

2. Using a boolean flag:
You can use a boolean flag to control the execution of the loop. Set the flag to false when you want to terminate the loop.
```csharp
bool executeLoop = true; // initialize flag

while (executeLoop)
{
    // your code here

    if (/* some condition */)
    {
        executeLoop = false; // set flag to false to terminate the loop
    }
}
```

3. Using a break statement:
Inside the loop, you can use a break statement to exit the loop based on a specific condition.
```csharp
while (true)
{
    // your code here

    if (/* some condition */)
    {
        break; // exit the loop
    }
}
```

These examples demonstrate different ways to limit the execution of a loop in C#. Choose the approach that best suits your requirements and logic.

