Here is an example of implementing the Builder design pattern in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Product class
class Pizza {
private:
    std::string dough;
    std::string sauce;
    std::string topping;

public:
    void setDough(const std::string& dough) {
        this->dough = dough;
    }

    void setSauce(const std::string& sauce) {
        this->sauce = sauce;
    }

    void setTopping(const std::string& topping) {
        this->topping = topping;
    }

    void describe() {
        std::cout << "Pizza with dough: " << dough
                  << ", sauce: " << sauce
                  << ", and topping: " << topping << std::endl;
    }
};

// Abstract builder
class PizzaBuilder {
protected:
    Pizza* pizza;

public:
    Pizza* getPizza() {
        return this->pizza;
    }

    void createNewPizza() {
        this->pizza = new Pizza();
    }

    virtual void buildDough() = 0;
    virtual void buildSauce() = 0;
    virtual void buildTopping() = 0;
};

// Concrete builder 1
class MargheritaPizzaBuilder : public PizzaBuilder {
public:
    void buildDough() override {
        pizza->setDough("regular");
    }

    void buildSauce() override {
        pizza->setSauce("tomato");
    }

    void buildTopping() override {
        pizza->setTopping("cheese");
    }
};

// Concrete builder 2
class PepperoniPizzaBuilder : public PizzaBuilder {
public:
    void buildDough() override {
        pizza->setDough("thin crust");
    }

    void buildSauce() override {
        pizza->setSauce("spicy");
    }

    void buildTopping() override {
        pizza->setTopping("pepperoni");
    }
};

// Director
class PizzaDirector {
private:
    PizzaBuilder* builder;

public:
    void setBuilder(PizzaBuilder* builder) {
        this->builder = builder;
    }

    Pizza* getPizza() {
        return builder->getPizza();
    }

    void constructPizza() {
        builder->createNewPizza();
        builder->buildDough();
        builder->buildSauce();
        builder->buildTopping();
    }
};

int main() {
    PizzaDirector director;
    MargheritaPizzaBuilder margheritaBuilder;
    PepperoniPizzaBuilder pepperoniBuilder;

    director.setBuilder(&margheritaBuilder);
    director.constructPizza();
    Pizza* margherita = director.getPizza();
    margherita->describe();

    director.setBuilder(&pepperoniBuilder);
    director.constructPizza();
    Pizza* pepperoni = director.getPizza();
    pepperoni->describe();

    delete margherita;
    delete pepperoni;

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we have a `Pizza` class as the product that the builder constructs. The `Pizza` class has setter methods to customize its attributes like dough, sauce, and topping.

The `PizzaBuilder` is an abstract base class that defines the interface for creating a pizza. It has pure virtual methods for building the dough, sauce, and topping. Each concrete builder (e.g., `MargheritaPizzaBuilder`, `PepperoniPizzaBuilder`) implements these methods to create different types of pizzas.

The `PizzaDirector` class is responsible for using a builder to construct a pizza. It has a `setBuilder` method to set the desired builder and a `constructPizza` method to execute the builder's methods in the proper order. Finally, the `getPizza` method is used to retrieve the constructed pizza.

In the `main` function, we create a `PizzaDirector` and two specific builders (`MargheritaPizzaBuilder`, `PepperoniPizzaBuilder`). We set the director's builder to the desired builder and call `constructPizza` to build the pizza. Finally, we retrieve the constructed pizza using `getPizza` and call the `describe` method to print its attributes.

Here is an example of implementing the Singleton design pattern in C++11 or later versions:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class Singleton {
private:
    static Singleton* instance;

    // Private constructor to prevent direct instantiation
    Singleton() {}

public:
    // Static method to retrieve the singleton instance
    static Singleton* getInstance() {
        if (instance == nullptr) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }

    // Public method
    void showMessage() {
        std::cout << "Hello, I am the singleton instance!" << std::endl;
    }
};

Singleton* Singleton::instance = nullptr;

int main() {
    // Singleton instance retrieved
    Singleton* singleton = Singleton::getInstance();

    // Call public methods
    singleton->showMessage();

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `Singleton` class has a private constructor to prevent direct instantiation of the class. The static member variable `instance` holds the single instance of the class.

The `getInstance` is a static method that ensures only one instance of the class exists. It checks if the `instance` variable is `nullptr` and creates a new object if it is. If an instance already exists, it returns the existing instance.

In the `main` function, we retrieve the singleton instance using `getInstance` method and call its public method `showMessage` to showcase the functionality of the singleton.

