Sure! I'll explain the structure of a Service Oriented Architecture (SOA) that includes entities, repositories, and controllers. I'll also connect these concepts to a general HTTP API web application.

In a Service Oriented Architecture, the main idea is to design your application as a collection of loosely coupled services that communicate with each other over a network. These services can be independent and can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently, which leads to better modularity and flexibility.

1. Entities: Entities represent the core data or objects in your application. They are the business objects that encapsulate the relevant information required by your application. For example, in an e-commerce application, entities can include User, Product, Order, etc. Entities typically have properties that describe their characteristics and behavior.

Example entity class (in Java):
```java
public class User {
    private String id;
    private String firstName;
    private String lastName;
    // ... other properties and methods
}
```

2. Repository: A repository is responsible for managing the persistence and retrieval of entities. It acts as an abstraction layer between your application and the data storage (such as a database). The repository provides methods to create, read, update, and delete entities from the underlying data storage.

Example repository interface (in Java):
```java
public interface UserRepository {
    User getById(String id);
    void save(User user);
    void delete(User user);
    // ... other methods for querying and manipulating user data
}
```

3. Service: A service is a business logic component that provides a well-defined set of functionalities related to a specific domain. It encapsulates the core logic and orchestrates the interactions between entities and repositories. Services use repositories to retrieve and persist data from/to the underlying storage.

Example service class (in Java):
```java
public class UserService {
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }

    public User getUser(String id) {
        return userRepository.getById(id);
    }

    public void createUser(User user) {
        // Perform validation or additional business logic
        userRepository.save(user);
    }

    public void deleteUser(String id) {
        User user = userRepository.getById(id);
        if (user != null) {
            // Perform additional checks or business logic
            userRepository.delete(user);
        }
    }

    // ... other service methods for manipulating user data
}
```

4. Controller: In the context of a web application, a controller handles incoming HTTP requests, performs the necessary operations, and returns an appropriate HTTP response. It acts as a bridge between the external world (web APIs) and the services. Controllers receive requests, extract the relevant data, invoke the appropriate service methods, and return the response.

Example controller class (in Java and Spring framework):
```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {
    private UserService userService;

    public UserController(UserService userService) {
        this.userService = userService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<User> getUserById(@PathVariable String id) {
        User user = userService.getUser(id);
        if (user != null) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok(user);
        } else {
            return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        }
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Void> createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        userService.createUser(user);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/users/" + user.getId())).build();
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> deleteUser(@PathVariable String id) {
        userService.deleteUser(id);
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }

    // ... other controller methods for handling user-related requests
}
```

By connecting these concepts to a general HTTP API web application, you can see that the controller handles incoming API requests and delegates the relevant operations to the service layer. The service layer, in turn, interacts with the repositories to retrieve and persist entity data.

With an HTTP API approach, you can expose the functionality of your application as a set of endpoints, thereby enabling other external systems or clients to consume your services over the internet using the standard HTTP protocol. These endpoints can follow the REST architectural style, where each endpoint represents a resource (e.g., users, products) and supports different HTTP verbs for different operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.).

