In the context of an e-commerce application, a product object represents a specific item that is available for sale. It could be a shirt, as you mentioned, or any other type of product. 

To model different types of products, you can use inheritance. For example, you could have a base Product class and then have specific classes such as Shirt, Pants, or Shoes that extend the Product class. Each specific product class can have additional properties or methods that are unique to that type.

Here's an example to illustrate the concept:

```java
public class Product {
    private String id;
    private String name;
    // ... common properties and methods applicable to all products
}

public class Shirt extends Product {
    private String size;
    // ... additional properties and methods specific to shirts
}

public class Pants extends Product {
    private int waistSize;
    private int inseam;
    // ... additional properties and methods specific to pants
}
```

In this example, Shirt is a specific type of Product that has an additional property called "size", which is specific to shirts. Similarly, Pants is another specific type of Product with its own additional properties.

Now, coming to the repository, the ProductRepository would be responsible for handling operations related to general product objects. It can provide methods like getProductById, saveProduct, updateProduct, and so on, which operate on the base Product class.

If you have additional functionality specific to shirts, such as retrieving shirts of a particular color, you could create a separate ShirtRepository that extends the ProductRepository. The ShirtRepository would provide additional methods specific to shirts, such as getShirtsByColor or getShirtsBySize.

Here's an example illustrating this:

```java
public interface ProductRepository {
    Product getProductById(String id);
    void saveProduct(Product product);
    // ... other methods for general product operations
}

public interface ShirtRepository extends ProductRepository {
    List<Shirt> getShirtsByColor(String color);
    List<Shirt> getShirtsBySize(String size);
    // ... other methods specific to shirts
}
```

By creating separate repositories for specialized functionalities, you can modularize your code and keep it organized. The ProductRepository can handle basic CRUD operations for all types of products, while the ShirtRepository can provide additional methods specifically for working with shirts.

This approach allows you to have a more granular control over querying and manipulating different types of products, based on their specific properties and characteristics.

