In Java, the switch statement is a control flow statement that allows you to select one of many code blocks to be executed based on the value of an expression. It provides a more organized and concise way to handle multiple possible values compared to using multiple if-else statements.

The syntax for a switch statement in Java is as follows:

```java
switch (expression) {
    case value1:
        // Code block to be executed if the expression matches value1
        break;
    case value2:
        // Code block to be executed if the expression matches value2
        break;
    // and so on...
    default:
        // Code block to be executed if none of the cases match the expression
        break;
}
```

