To count all the colored cells in Google Sheets, you can use a script. Here are the steps:

1. Open your Google Sheets document.
2. Click on "Extensions" in the top menu and select "Apps Script" to open the script editor.
3. Delete any existing code in the editor and paste the following code:

```javascript
function countColoredCells() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getDataRange();
  var coloredCellCount = 0;

  var fontColors = dataRange.getFontColors();
  var backgrounds = dataRange.getBackgrounds();

  for (var i = 0; i < backgrounds.length; i++) {
    for (var j = 0; j < backgrounds[0].length; j++) {
      if (backgrounds[i][j] != "#ffffff" || fontColors[i][j] != "#000000") {
        coloredCellCount++;
      }
    }
  }
  return coloredCellCount;
}
```

4. Save the script by clicking on the floppy disk icon or pressing "Ctrl + S".
5. Close the script editor.
6. To count all the colored cells, type the following formula in any cell of your Google Sheets document: `=countColoredCells()`.
7. The formula will calculate and display the count of all the colored cells in the sheet.

Note: This script counts both cells with background color and cells with font color. If you only want to count cells with background color, modify the `if` condition in the code to compare only `backgrounds[i][j] != "#ffffff"`.

To generate a random string with a length of 10 in .NET Core, you can use the `System.Random` class along with a combination of characters from which the string can be composed. Here's an example code snippet:

```csharp
using System;

public class RandomStringGenerator
{
    private static readonly Random random = new Random();

    public static string GenerateRandomString(int length)
    {
        const string chars = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789";
        return new string(Enumerable.Repeat(chars, length)
          .Select(s => s[random.Next(s.Length)]).ToArray());
    }
}
