To get a common object from a Proxy object in JavaScript, you can use the `Object.fromEntries()` method along with the `Object.entries()` method. Here's an example:

```javascript
const proxyObj = new Proxy(
  {
    inconsistencies: [1, 2, 3, 4],
    inferred: [2, 3, 4, 5]
  },
  {}
);

const commonObject = Object.fromEntries(
  Object.entries(proxyObj).reduce((acc, [key, value]) => {
    // Check if the value is an array
    if (Array.isArray(value)) {
      // Get the common elements between the arrays
      const commonElements = value.filter(element =>
        proxyObj.inferred.includes(element)
      );
      // Add the key-value pair to the accumulator array
      acc.push([key, commonElements]);
    }
    return acc;
  }, [])
);

console.log(commonObject);
```

In this example, we create a Proxy object `proxyObj` with two properties `inconsistencies` and `inferred`, which are arrays. We then use `Object.entries()` to get an array of key-value pairs from the Proxy object. We iterate over each key-value pair using `reduce()` and check if the value is an array. If it is an array, we filter the common elements between the array and the `proxyObj.inferred` array. Finally, we create a common object using `Object.fromEntries()` by converting the filtered key-value pairs array.

The resulting `commonObject` will only contain the common elements between the `inconsistencies` and `inferred` arrays.

