Yes, Hibernate will still be able to persist the data that is labeled as an entity, including its fields which are not labeled as entities. Hibernate treats the non-entity fields as basic types or value types.

When an entity has fields of other classes that are not themselves labeled as entities, Hibernate will use a technique called "Component Mapping" or "Embedded Mapping". This allows you to map composite or embedded objects as part of the entity's state and have them persist in the same table as the entity.

To achieve this, you can use annotations like `@Embeddable` and `@Embedded` in Hibernate. The `@Embeddable` annotation is used to mark the class whose fields will be embedded into the entity, and the `@Embedded` annotation is used to specify the embedded object within the entity.

Here's an example:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "my_entity")
public class MyEntity {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @Embedded
    private OtherClass other;

    // getters and setters
}

@Embeddable
public class OtherClass {

    private String field1;
    private String field2;

    // getters and setters
}
```

In this example, `MyEntity` is labeled as an entity, while `OtherClass` is not. However, the `OtherClass` is embedded within `MyEntity` using the `@Embedded` annotation.

When you save or update an instance of `MyEntity`, Hibernate will persist the data of the entity as well as the embedded `OtherClass` fields.

