        {
            transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPosition, targetPosition,
                Mathf.Clamp01(fractionOfJourney));
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we have a `speed` variable that determines the speed at which the object travels in units per second, a `distanceToTravel` variable representing the desired distance to be covered by the object, and a `timeToTravel` variable representing the specified time range for the object to reach its destination.

In the `Start()` method, we initialize the start position of the object (`startPosition`) and compute the target position (`targetPosition`) by adding the distance to be traveled to the start position. We also store the current time (`startTime`).

In the `Update()` method, we calculate the total length of the journey (`journeyLength`) using `Vector3.Distance` to determine the distance between the start and target positions. We also set the `journeyDuration` variable to the specified time range (`timeToTravel`).

Next, we calculate the distance covered by the object, the fraction of the journey completed, and the current duration since the start time using the same logic as before.

Finally, we check if the fraction of the journey is <= 1 (i.e., the object has not reached the destination) and if the current duration is within the specified time range. If both conditions are met, we use `Vector3.Lerp` to smoothly move the object from the start position to the target position based on the fraction of the journey completed.

Remember to attach this script to the object you want to move and adjust the `speed`, `distanceToTravel`, and `timeToTravel` variables according to your needs.

