To add a new element to an array in Java, you can follow these steps:

1. Declare an array variable and initialize it with the desired size. For example, to declare an array of integers called "arr" with a size of 5:
```java
int[] arr = new int[5];
```

2. Assign values to the existing elements of the array, if any. For example:
```java
arr[0] = 1;
arr[1] = 2;
arr[2] = 3;
arr[3] = 4;
arr[4] = 5;
```

3. Create a new array with a larger size and copy the existing elements to it. For example, to add a new element to the array:
```java
int newSize = arr.length + 1;
int[] newArr = new int[newSize];

for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    newArr[i] = arr[i];
}
```

4. Assign the new element to the last index of the new array. For example:
```java
int newElement = 6;
newArr[newSize - 1] = newElement;
```

5. Update the existing array variable to reference the new array. For example:
```java
arr = newArr;
```

After these steps, the original array "arr" will have a new element in it. Note that this approach creates a new array and copies the elements, which can be inefficient for large arrays. Alternatively, you can use data structures like ArrayList or LinkedList that allow dynamic resizing and provide methods to easily add new elements.

