Here's an example implementation of a Library class in Java that includes the addBook, borrowBook, and returnBook methods along with error handling and nested control structures:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Library {
    private Map<String, Integer> books;

    public Library() {
        books = new HashMap<>();
    }

    public void addBook(String title, String author, int quantity) {
        if (books.containsKey(title)) {
            int currentQuantity = books.get(title);
            books.put(title, currentQuantity + quantity);
        } else {
            books.put(title, quantity);
        }
        System.out.println("Book added: " + title + " by " + author);
    }

    public void borrowBook(String title, int quantity) {
        if (books.containsKey(title)) {
            int availableQuantity = books.get(title);
            if (availableQuantity >= quantity) {
                books.put(title, availableQuantity - quantity);
                System.out.println("Successfully borrowed " + quantity + " copies of " + title);
            } else {
                System.out.println("Not enough copies of " + title + " available");
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println("Book not found: " + title);
        }
    }

    public void returnBook(String title, int quantity) {
        if (books.containsKey(title)) {
            int borrowedQuantity = books.get(title);
            if (borrowedQuantity >= quantity) {
                books.put(title, borrowedQuantity + quantity);
                System.out.println("Successfully returned " + quantity + " copies of " + title);
            } else {
                System.out.println("Invalid return quantity for " + title);
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println("Book not found: " + title);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Library library = new Library();

        library.addBook("Java Programming", "John Doe", 10);
        library.addBook("Python Basics", "Jane Smith", 5);

        library.borrowBook("Java Programming", 4);
        library.borrowBook("Python Basics", 3);
        library.borrowBook("C++ Fundamentals", 2); // Error: Book not found

        library.returnBook("Java Programming", 7); // Error: Invalid return quantity
        library.returnBook("Python Basics", 2);
        library.returnBook("C++ Fundamentals", 1); // Error: Book not found
        library.returnBook("Java Programming", 3);

        // Check library book quantities
        System.out.println("Book quantities:");
        for (String title : library.books.keySet()) {
            System.out.println(title + ": " + library.books.get(title));
        }
    }
}
```

This program creates a Library class with a map to store book titles and quantities. The `addBook` method updates the quantity if the book already exists or adds a new entry. The `borrowBook` method checks if the requested books are available and updates the quantity accordingly. The `returnBook` method checks if the returned books exist and updates the quantity. Error messages are displayed for invalid scenarios.

The `main` method demonstrates the usage of the Library class by adding books, borrowing and returning books, and displaying the book quantities.

